1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
As I am sure everyone else feels the same way, no better place to be at 7.20pm through 10pm on a Monday night.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,000
And so we will proceed.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
So we finished up with signaling last week.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Actually you could spend many years studying signaling if you were so inclined.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
But this is all that we're going to do in this class on the subject.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
In my view there's still plenty more to do on it, so we shall see.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,000
But, and then we got a last topic.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,000
So the winner's curse.

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Another fun and kind of surprising case where economists did actually add something to practical decision making.

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Not surprisingly.

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Moral hazard, like I said, everyone in church knew about moral hazard before economists started talking about it.

12
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Adverse selection, probably a bit more influence from economics.

13
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
But the winner's curse and other things, economists actually said something.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,000
So, simple case.

15
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,000
So suppose we start with a second price auction.

16
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:13,000
A second price auction is one where the highest bidder wins and he pays the bid of the second highest bidder.

17
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:21,000
So if the bids are 10, 9, and 5, who wins?

18
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
What is the money bid 10 and the winner pays how much?

19
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:25,000
9.

20
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
That's a second price auction.

21
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:29,000
And if this were a first price auction, who would win?

22
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:30,000
10.

23
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:31,000
And what would he pay?

24
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:32,000
10.

25
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:33,000
10.

26
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Now you may immediately realize there are different incentives for your bidding in a first versus second price auction.

27
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,000
So in a first price auction, if you truly value 10, do you want to bid 10?

28
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Probably not, because then even if you win you get no surplus.

29
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,000
So you don't want to do that.

30
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
On the other hand, as you're going to prove on the homework, if it's a second price auction,

31
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:55,000
then normally it makes sense to just bid your true value.

32
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Because you don't pay your true value.

33
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
You pay the value of the second highest person.

34
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,000
And you'll actually get a chance to prove that is a very good strategy.

35
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Normally.

36
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Yes.

37
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:13,000
So like if I want to blow up the boat and the guy bids 9, I could bid like 1,000.

38
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Yeah, exactly.

39
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:15,000
I don't think he's going to bid 1,000 once.

40
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Yeah, you could bid 10K and you still pay 9.

41
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Because I can drop out, right?

42
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,000
If he bids 1,000, I can drop out.

43
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
So hold on, this is a first or second price auction?

44
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
This is a second price auction.

45
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Yeah, second price auction.

46
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:27,000
But yeah, I mean you get the idea.

47
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:32,000
I could just bid 1,000 and then like assuming the guy is not going to want to bid 1,000 once,

48
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
he's going to have to pay at least 1,000.

49
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Yeah, exactly.

50
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
So again, normally we picture this as sealed bids where you just write your number on a piece of paper

51
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:41,000
and then we sort them out.

52
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:47,000
But you could just as easily imagine that it's an auction where we raise the price by a penny a time

53
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
until finally there's only one person left.

54
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
And notice that's the same thing, right, for all practical purposes.

55
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,000
So again, imagine that we just start the price at zero and then we raise it by a penny per return

56
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:05,000
and then we finally get to $5.01 and this guy drops out, right?

57
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,000
And then we raise it up and we get to $9.01 and then this guy drops out

58
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,000
and then this person here pays $9.01.

59
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Once there's only one person left, then that person wins.

60
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
So it's just the difference of a penny.

61
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
So anyway, first price auction is the one that we usually immediately think of

62
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:24,000
where you pay whatever you bid.

63
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:29,000
But second price auction is also effectively in pretty wide use

64
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:34,000
because any time you just raise the amount in small increments until only one person is left,

65
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
that's virtually the same as a second price auction.

66
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Might make sense.

67
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Okay.

68
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
So imagine we got a second price auction and we got N participants.

69
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Now, of course there's many other fun auction rules.

70
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:54,000
There is the all-pay auction where everyone has to pay whatever they bid.

71
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
Some highest bidder wins, but everyone pays whatever they bid.

72
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:01,000
So all the people who don't win pay their money and then get nothing.

73
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
You can do that.

74
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Why would anyone participate in an all-pay auction?

75
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Bids are likely to be lower.

76
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,000
Yeah, bids tend to be really low in all-pay auctions,

77
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:16,000
so maybe you will get what you want for pennies on the door.

78
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Of course there's all the losers, but you always take a chance there.

79
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Okay.

80
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Now, we got N participants,

81
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
and then let's suppose that every bidder has rational expectations

82
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
about the true value of the item being auctioned.

83
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:34,000
So what does this mean?

84
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Well, it's something like an oil well, right,

85
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
where you're at least allowed to drill in an area and find the oil,

86
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,000
where the value to each person there is not based upon a personal interpretation

87
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:46,000
of the joy of the oil, right?

88
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:50,000
It's just based upon what is the market price of the oil that is below that ground.

89
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Similarly, this can even be true for art collecting.

90
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
If we suppose that you are all investors in art,

91
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
so you're not buying it for enjoyment.

92
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,000
You're buying it to sell on the market at some point.

93
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Okay, so that would work too.

94
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:03,000
All right.

95
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:06,000
So suppose that everybody goes and has rational expectations

96
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,000
about the true value of the auction.

97
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
So everyone is going to have their own valuation.

98
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,000
So your own valuation is VI, which is an equal to V,

99
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:18,000
which is the true value, plus your own personal error term,

100
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,000
your own random mistake, which could be high or low.

101
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Okay.

102
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
And then since we got rational expectations,

103
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
assume that the mean of the error is zero,

104
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:31,000
and it's got a variance of sigma squared.

105
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Okay.

106
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,000
Now, since your estimate is unbiased by assumption,

107
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:40,000
it seems like the sensible thing is just to bid your true value.

108
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:43,000
As I said, on the homework you'll get a chance to prove this.

109
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
But intuitively, suppose that, you know,

110
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:52,000
let's go back to, you know, so we got like true values are 10, 9, and 5.

111
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
These are true values.

112
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Now, so this person here is, you know,

113
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
suppose this person here, they go and bid 10.

114
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:00,000
All right.

115
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,000
And then, of course, they're going to win,

116
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
because this person is not going to bid any more.

117
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
There's no reason to bid more than your true value, right?

118
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:07,000
Right.

119
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
If you bid more than your true value, then there's two scenarios.

120
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
One is you lose, in which case what happens?

121
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Zero.

122
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Nothing.

123
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Right.

124
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:16,000
Easy come, easy go.

125
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
On the other hand, if you bid more than true value and you win,

126
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,000
what has just occurred?

127
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:20,000
You lost.

128
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:21,000
You lost.

129
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Yes.

130
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:25,000
You have won the auction but lost the value race,

131
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:29,000
because you have paid, say, $9.50 for something you only vetted at $9.00.

132
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:30,000
That's stupid.

133
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
And that's the second highest price below your true value.

134
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:33,000
Yes.

135
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
I said, either you lose, in which case you're fine.

136
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Right.

137
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Or you win, in which case you regret it.

138
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,000
Oh, I see.

139
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

140
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
You're absolutely right, which is why, yes, completely right,

141
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
which is why proving this is a little bit harder,

142
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
and that's why you get to do the homework.

143
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,000
Anyway.

144
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,000
All right.

145
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:55,000
So, anyway, so this person here,

146
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
so suppose that you're thinking about bidding $10.00,

147
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:03,000
and then there's a question of what do you gain if you go and cut your bid?

148
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:04,000
All right.

149
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
So, again, if you bid $10.00, and, you know,

150
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
the other person has no reason to go above $9.00,

151
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
so if you bid $10.00, then you're going to wind up paying $9.00.

152
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:13,000
Right.

153
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
Now, suppose you cut your bid down to $9.80.

154
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,000
Do you still win?

155
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,000
And what do you pay if you bid $9.80?

156
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:22,000
You still pay $9.00.

157
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,000
What if you go and you cut your bid to $9.50?

158
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:25,000
What do you pay?

159
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:26,000
$9.00.

160
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,000
What about if you cut your bid down to $9.01?

161
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:29,000
What do you pay?

162
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:30,000
$9.00.

163
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
What if you cut your bid down to $8.99?

164
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:34,000
Then what happens?

165
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:38,000
Then you lose, and you actually don't get the item.

166
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:39,000
Right.

167
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
On the other hand, if you were to go and raise your bid, what do you get?

168
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:44,000
Raising your bid doesn't get you anything either, right?

169
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,000
You could bid $1 million, and you still pay $9.00.

170
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,000
Right.

171
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:53,000
So, anyway, that's the basic intuition for why it actually is least weakly dominant

172
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
for you to bid your true value.

173
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:56,000
All right.

174
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:00,000
So, anyway, suppose that everyone goes and bids their true value, right,

175
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,000
and figuring, look, I've got rational expectations,

176
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:05,000
so if I bid my true value, what can go wrong?

177
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:06,000
What can go wrong?

178
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:10,000
And it turns out that, surprisingly, given the setup of this problem,

179
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:15,000
if you win, you should expect to lose money.

180
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:16,000
Why?

181
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:24,000
Well, so if this is your estimate, so the estimate is going to be your view of the value,

182
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:27,000
your best guess of the value, equal to the truth plus the error,

183
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:30,000
and you know everybody else is the same, right?

184
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,000
So if you look at this, who's actually going to win the auction?

185
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,000
The person with the highest error.

186
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,000
Yeah, the person with the biggest positive error is going to win the auction

187
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
if you've just bid your true value.

188
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:42,000
And then you lose a loss, right?

189
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:46,000
You basically lose, and you wind up paying that amount,

190
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:50,000
paying an amount equal to the true value plus the second biggest error.

191
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:51,000
Now, here's the thing.

192
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:57,000
The average error is zero, but the average error of the winner is positive,

193
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
and that is the danger.

194
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
That is the danger because if you, you know, so, of course, on average,

195
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:06,000
you just bid it and you're fine, but you just bid your true value,

196
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:12,000
and if your error is zero, as it is on average, you just almost certainly lose, right?

197
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,000
Because there's going to be someone with a positive error,

198
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
it could be a large group of people, right, a large group of bidders.

199
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:22,000
But if you win, on the other hand, you wind up overpaying.

200
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:28,000
Okay, so that is the problem that is known as the winner's curse.

201
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:32,000
Because if you win, you will wish you had lost.

202
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:35,000
If you win, you wish you had lost, but you know, be careful what you bid for,

203
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
you might get it.

204
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
So if you bid what seems like a good amount to you,

205
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,000
normally you just walk away with nothing,

206
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:46,000
but if you win it will be because you had a very high positive error,

207
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:49,000
and therefore you wind up overpaying because, again,

208
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,000
you will pay the value of the second most deluded person,

209
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
most overly optimistic person.

210
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:00,000
Does sellers having a negative error versus sellers just pricing low

211
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,000
to attract a number of buyers, or bidders rather,

212
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:05,000
does that come into this at all?

213
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
I mean, you could just, like,

214
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:10,000
principally you could reverse it and make this,

215
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
so this is all for ease of buyers.

216
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,000
You could just easily reverse it and make it sellers,

217
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:19,000
where as long as the seller doesn't know the true value of what they're selling,

218
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,000
then the problem is that the ones that are going to offer the lowest price

219
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:26,000
are going to be the ones where they are most likely to have underestimated

220
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
the cost of doing it.

221
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,000
So it's totally symmetric.

222
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,000
So the more serious your error, the more likely you are to win.

223
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
Where win does not mean make money.

224
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:40,000
Win means be declared the winner of the auction,

225
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
which are two quite different things, right?

226
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:46,000
Have you ever had the nightmare that you accidentally go to an auction

227
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:50,000
and you accidentally bid at the wrong time and then you owe $10 million?

228
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:54,000
How many Simpsons episodes are based on that?

229
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,000
I feel like there's been so many auctions on the Simpsons.

230
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:02,000
You just scratch your nose, but you accidentally put your bidding thing up,

231
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:05,000
and sold to this idiot over here.

232
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:09,000
You're like, what? Sold.

233
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
This was like three weeks ago.

234
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:14,000
There was a bad auction episode.

235
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
So that is the idea.

236
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:19,000
If you win, you're likely to make a serious error.

237
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
Now, what can you do about this?

238
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Well, if everybody knew everybody's VI's, then there's a really easy solution.

239
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
So you just take the average of all of them,

240
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
and then that gives you a better estimate of the truth.

241
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
So basically, if you could just go to every person and say,

242
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
what's your estimate of the true value? What's your estimate?

243
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:38,000
Everyone will be honest.

244
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
Then you just go and you take them all and average them all,

245
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:43,000
and then on average that's going to be an accurate judgment.

246
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:47,000
Of course, in the real world, you would not expect people to go around

247
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:52,000
and answer truthfully whatever they believe the value of the object is, right?

248
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:53,000
Okay?

249
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,000
Now, there is another thing that you can do.

250
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,000
Another thing you can do, which is,

251
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
so of course you could actually release all the model,

252
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
or you could just notice, well, wait,

253
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:07,000
on average how much does the winner overbid by?

254
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:10,000
Suppose that the winner overbids by an average of 20%

255
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
when they're doing it naively.

256
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:16,000
Then all that you could need to do is take your estimate

257
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
and say bid.8 of that.

258
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:19,000
Well, let's see, hold on.

259
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
I said they're bidding 20% too high.

260
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
So that's 6 fifths.

261
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
So actually you want to have 5 sixths.

262
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
So if you were just to go and bid 5 sixths,

263
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
then at least on average you would,

264
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:34,000
if you won, you would be glad you won.

265
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,000
We're going to be seeing how many of you bid 5 sixths of your estimate

266
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
that you're probably going to lose.

267
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:40,000
Say, well, you'll lose, yes, you will.

268
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
So if everyone else keeps doing it, you'll lose,

269
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
but you'll be glad you lost.

270
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
And then if it so happens that you win,

271
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
you will not be sad that you won.

272
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:53,000
So it's better to lose auctions but not money

273
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
than it is to win auctions but lose money.

274
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
So anyway, basically, so intuitively,

275
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:01,000
if you look and see the winner is typically losing money,

276
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:05,000
then the obvious thing to do is to take your initial naive estimate

277
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:12,000
and then deflate it so that you can once again expect to make money.

278
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
So, basic point.

279
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
And then if you do win, you will not be burned.

280
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:21,000
Of course, if everybody does this, then all the bids get depressed,

281
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:26,000
but then on average, the winner is going to be happy that he won.

282
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
If you deflate it enough.

283
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
Now, how does this work in the real world?

284
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,000
Well, so some economists went and looked at what was going on,

285
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:36,000
and specifically they did bidding on petroleum,

286
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:40,000
or like potential petroleum, so an area believed in petroleum,

287
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:45,000
and then they noticed there were patterns of persistent overbidding.

288
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:48,000
But then, before too long, what firms started doing was

289
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
not going and explaining this whole problem,

290
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:53,000
and it's not clear that they understood at all what was going on.

291
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:56,000
All they would do is come up with a naive estimate,

292
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,000
and then just subtract a third, and then did that.

293
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
And if you ask them, why do you do that?

294
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:04,000
And they say, well, we went and calculated our thing,

295
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
and then we just do our standard industry thing,

296
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:07,000
and subtracting a third at the end.

297
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
But why do you do that?

298
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:12,000
Well, when we don't subtract a third, we lose money.

299
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:17,000
So this is one where they atheoretically hit on the right answer

300
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:21,000
without having much of a clear ability to explain what's going on.

301
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:24,000
So, I mean, this is something where the economists did not show up

302
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
and give them a new business strategy,

303
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:30,000
but they were able to show up and explain this weird fudge factor

304
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
happening at the second to last step,

305
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:36,000
where you just go, well, let's just go and subtract this thing

306
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:40,000
that everyone knows you have to subtract, or else you lose money.

307
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:44,000
So that's how they worked it out in practice.

308
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:48,000
So, let's see.

309
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:49,000
Yeah.

310
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:53,000
So, the unrelated example, I remember in the state of New Jersey,

311
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:59,000
if you were hiring a mover, they would go and calculate out the rate,

312
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,000
and then they would go and subtract somewhere between 40 and 60 percent

313
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,000
from whatever their second to last number was.

314
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:08,000
And I remember when I was asking them what's going on there,

315
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:12,000
and they said, well, so this is an interstate move,

316
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
and so we're allowed to discount.

317
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:18,000
I'm like, okay, but why is it like 40 to 60 percent?

318
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
And they said, well, it depends upon demand.

319
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:24,000
So if it's a slack time, then we'll take off 60 percent.

320
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:28,000
If it's a regular time, like 50, peak time would be like 45 percent.

321
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:31,000
I'm like, why is it so big?

322
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
Anyway, so when I looked into this,

323
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:37,000
the state of New Jersey does actually fix the prices for moves.

324
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
But then as long as it's a move between states,

325
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
you're allowed to put a discount on.

326
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
But if it were intrastate, in principle,

327
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:48,000
they would have to actually legally charge whatever that high number was.

328
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:51,000
Now, it seems like a pretty crummy price control for it from anyone's point of view

329
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:55,000
because they basically self-assess each item, like how many boxes,

330
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
and it didn't seem like there was any system

331
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
to make sure they put down the right number of boxes,

332
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:02,000
and I think it depended upon the size of the boxes.

333
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:05,000
You could just go and change large to medium boxes

334
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
and then get the price down that way.

335
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:10,000
But anyway, this is another case where they do all the math,

336
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:14,000
and then the second to last step, enormous discount,

337
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:17,000
and then final number, and this is the easiest way to practice.

338
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:18,000
My question?

339
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:21,000
So the winner's curse does apply to like a first price auction as well, right?

340
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:24,000
Yeah, it's just easiest to see in the case of a second price auction.

341
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:29,000
And notice, it does not apply if it's just a variation on how much you like the product.

342
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:33,000
So if instead of epsilon i being an error,

343
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:36,000
instead it is just an amount of utility that you get,

344
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
then it's no problem.

345
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:41,000
So again, if the person that wins the Picasso auction

346
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
is the person who loves Picasso the most,

347
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
that's just a normal efficient outcome.

348
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:48,000
But what's interesting about this is we are actually assuming

349
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:51,000
that there's a true value that you're doing,

350
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:54,000
say, for a firm that just wants to resell the oil,

351
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:57,000
and then the problem is not variation in the value you assign,

352
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:02,000
but a variation in your estimate of how much oil is there.

353
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
All right.

354
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
And then if you read Lansbury's chapter on this,

355
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:09,000
which I think is assigned, I don't remember, but anyway,

356
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:13,000
he uncharacteristically misstates the theory,

357
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
possibly just for entertainment value,

358
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
but begins with, if you're reading this book,

359
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
you probably overestimated how good it is.

360
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:23,000
And the idea is that when you're looking around for books on the shelf,

361
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
like what are the ones, you know,

362
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
who are the people who are going to pick up

363
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
the Armchair Economist off the shelf right away?

364
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:33,000
Are they going to be people who accurately forecast its value?

365
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
Are they going to be the optimists?

366
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
That sounds great, and then you get it.

367
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:40,000
But anyway, that's only if you act naively,

368
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:46,000
but on the other hand, if you realize that whatever you're picking up

369
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:49,000
will tend to be worse than you expected,

370
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:50,000
then you will make an adjustment,

371
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:53,000
lower your expectations down to a reasonable level,

372
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:57,000
and then you would not actually be disappointed by the results.

373
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
All right, so you can make your own judgment

374
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
about whether you're good at this.

375
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
Have you successfully lowered your expectations

376
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,000
down to a realistic level by this point in your life?

377
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:09,000
When you're a kid, like probably most people do,

378
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:12,000
most kids do have an overly optimistic view

379
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
of how much they're going to enjoy almost everything,

380
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:18,000
and then things can get pretty disappointing for those kids.

381
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
I thought this was going to be the best birthday ever,

382
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:23,000
but it's only like the third best birthday.

383
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
All right.

384
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:29,000
All right, so questions about The Winner's Curse?

385
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:34,000
Are you completely happy about The Winner's Curse and everything about it?

386
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
Yes, happy about it.

387
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:40,000
Intellectually satisfied by The Winner's Curse.

388
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:45,000
All right, in that case, we'll move on to efficiency implications.

389
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:48,000
So as I said, there are plenty of e-contact books,

390
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
and I think they're still being written,

391
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:53,000
that say that when there's imperfect information, markets fail.

392
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:56,000
And this is a gross overstatement and demonstrably wrong.

393
00:18:56,000 --> 00:19:00,000
It's like weird GMU lore that says that it's wrong.

394
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:04,000
But there are ironclad counter-examples

395
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:07,000
that every economist who can do math will say,

396
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:10,000
yes, that is a counter-example of the claim that markets fail

397
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:14,000
when there's imperfect information, so that claim is wrong.

398
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:15,000
All right, so general rules.

399
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:18,000
First, if the imperfect information is symmetric,

400
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
so we've got symmetric imperfect information

401
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:23,000
where nobody knows any better than anybody else,

402
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
then we've got market efficiency.

403
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:31,000
At least in the sense that there's nothing that anyone can do to improve anything.

404
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:36,000
All right, now, what if all market agents are equally informed,

405
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:40,000
but the government knows more than all the people in the market?

406
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
Is that symmetric or asymmetric?

407
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:47,000
It's asymmetric, because to us, there's got to be anybody in the universe.

408
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:51,000
So if the government knows better than all the people in the market,

409
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
that would be a case of asymmetric information.

410
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:56,000
And you might very well say, well, of course, whenever a government says

411
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
there's a problem with imperfect information,

412
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:23,000
that's because the government knows better.


1
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:03,760
The government knows better, or at least claims to know better.

2
00:20:03,760 --> 00:20:06,600
Isn't it odd for the government to go and try

3
00:20:06,600 --> 00:20:09,560
to fix an information problem when it doesn't know any better

4
00:20:09,560 --> 00:20:11,880
than anybody else?

5
00:20:11,880 --> 00:20:13,740
Now, if this is true, so if it's true

6
00:20:13,740 --> 00:20:15,320
that the problem is simply that there's

7
00:20:15,320 --> 00:20:17,120
asymmetric information but the government has

8
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:22,240
extra information, then there's a really simple, light-handed

9
00:20:22,240 --> 00:20:24,000
intervention government can do.

10
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:28,880
And this is publicly reveal what it knows.

11
00:20:28,880 --> 00:20:32,080
So the government can solve the asymmetric information problem

12
00:20:32,080 --> 00:20:35,440
by giving away its information.

13
00:20:35,440 --> 00:20:37,320
And you say, who would believe the government?

14
00:20:37,320 --> 00:20:38,700
Well, if the government is really

15
00:20:38,700 --> 00:20:42,320
so noble in its efforts and so vast in its understanding,

16
00:20:42,320 --> 00:20:44,760
then people would be hanging on the government's every word

17
00:20:44,760 --> 00:20:46,320
to go, oh my god, the government says

18
00:20:46,320 --> 00:20:47,840
that these cars are a bad deal.

19
00:20:47,840 --> 00:20:51,200
I don't want them anymore.

20
00:20:51,200 --> 00:20:53,560
Now, you may notice out of all of the discussion

21
00:20:53,560 --> 00:20:55,800
about the opioid epidemic, what is

22
00:20:55,800 --> 00:21:00,600
one thing we pretty much never heard the government say?

23
00:21:00,600 --> 00:21:02,480
Any government agents here?

24
00:21:02,480 --> 00:21:04,320
Maybe they do it, but probably they actually

25
00:21:04,320 --> 00:21:05,480
do it in K through 12.

26
00:21:05,480 --> 00:21:07,400
And this is, the government has determined

27
00:21:07,400 --> 00:21:08,360
this is really dangerous.

28
00:21:08,360 --> 00:21:10,480
And you don't know this yet, but we're telling you,

29
00:21:10,480 --> 00:21:12,640
it's dangerous.

30
00:21:12,640 --> 00:21:13,960
So that could be yours.

31
00:21:13,960 --> 00:21:16,040
What is the solution to the opioid epidemic?

32
00:21:16,040 --> 00:21:18,720
Just the president gets on the news and my fellow Americans,

33
00:21:18,720 --> 00:21:20,220
the government has looked into this,

34
00:21:20,220 --> 00:21:22,440
and we found that these drugs are dangerous.

35
00:21:22,440 --> 00:21:25,880
One in 10, more and more are more dangerous than you think.

36
00:21:25,880 --> 00:21:28,520
OK, all right, carry on.

37
00:21:28,520 --> 00:21:30,640
Problem solved.

38
00:21:30,640 --> 00:21:32,040
Just you.

39
00:21:32,040 --> 00:21:33,600
Yeah, give them a ceiling.

40
00:21:33,600 --> 00:21:35,480
All right, we solved that.

41
00:21:35,480 --> 00:21:37,080
We've revealed this information.

42
00:21:37,080 --> 00:21:39,680
And of course, they could get more detail saying, look,

43
00:21:39,680 --> 00:21:42,680
according to our calculations, there's a 3% chance

44
00:21:42,680 --> 00:21:45,080
that if you use these drugs regularly for the next five

45
00:21:45,080 --> 00:21:47,200
years, you will die.

46
00:21:47,200 --> 00:21:48,720
Just thought you ought to know.

47
00:21:48,720 --> 00:21:50,200
I know I'm dorky.

48
00:21:50,200 --> 00:21:53,960
Yeah, we have a question back there.

49
00:21:53,960 --> 00:21:55,520
All right.

50
00:21:55,520 --> 00:21:59,360
So in this case, the government reestablishes

51
00:21:59,360 --> 00:22:01,720
symmetric information.

52
00:22:01,720 --> 00:22:05,520
And so no need to do more.

53
00:22:05,520 --> 00:22:08,240
Now, there are plenty of times where government

54
00:22:08,240 --> 00:22:10,920
does go and warn people.

55
00:22:10,920 --> 00:22:14,240
Although it's not that common that they stop at that.

56
00:22:14,240 --> 00:22:17,320
But this is just, we're just sharing this information.

57
00:22:17,320 --> 00:22:19,240
So the Surgery General has determined

58
00:22:19,240 --> 00:22:21,360
that cigarette smoking is hazardous to your health.

59
00:22:21,360 --> 00:22:22,680
Put that warning there.

60
00:22:22,680 --> 00:22:25,160
And now you know what the government doesn't know.

61
00:22:25,160 --> 00:22:28,800
Now you know what the government knows.

62
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:33,800
All right, so that is simple stage.

63
00:22:33,800 --> 00:22:36,720
Now, if you've got asymmetric information,

64
00:22:36,720 --> 00:22:39,960
then there's going to be some efficiency implications,

65
00:22:39,960 --> 00:22:42,080
as we've seen.

66
00:22:42,080 --> 00:22:43,400
But what are they?

67
00:22:43,400 --> 00:22:46,560
Well, so even when market outcomes are inefficient,

68
00:22:46,560 --> 00:22:48,320
government may be unable to prove matters.

69
00:22:48,320 --> 00:22:49,560
So moral hazard.

70
00:22:49,560 --> 00:22:53,120
Here there is a general problem with asymmetric information.

71
00:22:53,120 --> 00:22:55,600
And you do get inefficient results with moral hazard.

72
00:22:55,600 --> 00:22:57,040
But what exactly is the government

73
00:22:57,040 --> 00:22:59,120
supposed to do about it?

74
00:22:59,120 --> 00:23:02,640
What's the government supposed to do about it?

75
00:23:02,640 --> 00:23:04,720
If you get creative, you can think about things.

76
00:23:04,720 --> 00:23:07,440
How about jailing people for lying about whether they smoke

77
00:23:07,440 --> 00:23:10,320
or they apply for health insurance?

78
00:23:10,320 --> 00:23:11,640
Do that.

79
00:23:11,640 --> 00:23:12,640
All right, what's that?

80
00:23:12,640 --> 00:23:15,800
What moral hazard problem does that solve?

81
00:23:15,800 --> 00:23:18,360
People lying about smoking.

82
00:23:18,360 --> 00:23:20,480
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

83
00:23:20,480 --> 00:23:22,360
People don't lie about smoking anymore.

84
00:23:22,360 --> 00:23:23,760
Now, just put a warning up there.

85
00:23:23,760 --> 00:23:24,840
Now you're going to do it.

86
00:23:24,840 --> 00:23:25,880
You're going to reveal it.

87
00:23:25,880 --> 00:23:29,600
So you could do that kind of thing.

88
00:23:29,600 --> 00:23:30,880
Or let's see.

89
00:23:30,880 --> 00:23:32,040
There's other things.

90
00:23:32,040 --> 00:23:33,880
So you could actually have the government

91
00:23:33,880 --> 00:23:37,280
could go and tax unhealthy behaviors.

92
00:23:37,280 --> 00:23:39,740
Now, again, of course, if the government couldn't find out,

93
00:23:39,740 --> 00:23:40,880
there'd be a problem.

94
00:23:40,880 --> 00:23:42,600
The government could actually mandate

95
00:23:42,600 --> 00:23:44,120
that people go and be monitored.

96
00:23:44,120 --> 00:23:47,480
Or you could go and use facial recognition technology

97
00:23:47,480 --> 00:23:49,400
and then immediately funnel all the information

98
00:23:49,400 --> 00:23:52,560
to insurance companies so they know whether you're lying.

99
00:23:52,560 --> 00:23:55,760
This is a really popular idea, right?

100
00:23:55,760 --> 00:23:57,480
All right, secure the market failure.

101
00:23:57,480 --> 00:23:59,320
We now are going to tell insurance companies

102
00:23:59,320 --> 00:24:01,400
everything about you.

103
00:24:01,400 --> 00:24:06,600
So again, not very popular, as you might have heard.

104
00:24:06,600 --> 00:24:10,840
By the way, so there is not only a popular but also

105
00:24:10,840 --> 00:24:14,600
a legal interest in preserving personal privacy.

106
00:24:14,600 --> 00:24:17,640
And what is privacy, really?

107
00:24:17,640 --> 00:24:19,280
Privacy is asymmetric information,

108
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:22,160
which we've got a bunch of models saying this potentially

109
00:24:22,160 --> 00:24:23,480
leads to inefficient results.

110
00:24:23,480 --> 00:24:26,480
What's so great about it?

111
00:24:26,480 --> 00:24:29,720
And most of the arguments for privacy,

112
00:24:29,720 --> 00:24:33,840
the simple arguments anyway, are kind of odd because, well,

113
00:24:33,840 --> 00:24:38,160
so we all have a secret to hide, don't we?

114
00:24:38,160 --> 00:24:40,720
But who are we hiding it from?

115
00:24:40,720 --> 00:24:43,600
Wouldn't that person be better off if they knew?

116
00:24:43,600 --> 00:24:46,320
So who's to say that it would be better

117
00:24:46,320 --> 00:24:47,640
if everyone keeps their secrets?

118
00:24:47,640 --> 00:24:50,880
Maybe better if everyone's secrets were released.

119
00:24:50,880 --> 00:24:53,960
What are you trying to hide?

120
00:24:53,960 --> 00:24:57,200
So at least we're thinking about.

121
00:24:57,200 --> 00:24:59,760
So you could develop a more complicated economic model,

122
00:24:59,760 --> 00:25:01,280
for example.

123
00:25:01,280 --> 00:25:03,320
Maybe there's just pure embarrassment.

124
00:25:03,320 --> 00:25:05,720
So maybe people are giving you secrets just because they

125
00:25:05,720 --> 00:25:06,400
get embarrassed.

126
00:25:06,400 --> 00:25:07,480
Other people find out.

127
00:25:07,480 --> 00:25:09,920
It's not that you actually are trying

128
00:25:09,920 --> 00:25:12,720
to take advantage of anyone else or anything like that.

129
00:25:12,720 --> 00:25:14,800
We all would feel better if no one

130
00:25:14,800 --> 00:25:17,440
could know what we're thinking all the time.

131
00:25:17,440 --> 00:25:18,160
That's possible.

132
00:25:18,160 --> 00:25:20,560
But on the other hand, a lot of the secrets people really

133
00:25:20,560 --> 00:25:22,520
don't want to be found are the kind of thing

134
00:25:22,520 --> 00:25:24,840
that a private investigator would be hired

135
00:25:24,840 --> 00:25:26,040
by somebody else to discover.

136
00:25:30,160 --> 00:25:34,280
Anyway, so moral hazard is one where usually government just

137
00:25:34,280 --> 00:25:35,000
gives up on it.

138
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:39,320
Or as I said in the case of Obamacare,

139
00:25:39,320 --> 00:25:41,880
one obvious effect of Obamacare is to make moral hazard worse.

140
00:25:41,880 --> 00:25:44,760
And what are the remedies or anything?

141
00:25:44,760 --> 00:25:46,760
So the remedy is live with it.

142
00:25:46,760 --> 00:25:48,800
Live with worse incentives.

143
00:25:48,800 --> 00:25:52,880
Sounds not really much of a solution at all, is it?

144
00:25:52,880 --> 00:25:54,600
Now finally, there are a lot of cases

145
00:25:54,600 --> 00:25:56,140
where government can improve matters,

146
00:25:56,140 --> 00:25:59,000
but actual regulations do the opposite.

147
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:01,080
And this is, again, something worth knowing.

148
00:26:04,080 --> 00:26:05,960
So we're on this list.

149
00:26:05,960 --> 00:26:08,520
So limiting contractual punishment.

150
00:26:08,520 --> 00:26:10,120
So as I said, contractual punishment,

151
00:26:10,120 --> 00:26:12,000
this is where you sign a contract where

152
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,320
you say, if I am caught doing something bad,

153
00:26:14,320 --> 00:26:15,200
I will pay a fine.

154
00:26:17,800 --> 00:26:21,800
And this is actually a way of dealing with moral hazard,

155
00:26:21,800 --> 00:26:25,480
first of all, because when you sign that contract,

156
00:26:25,480 --> 00:26:26,800
what are you doing to yourself?

157
00:26:26,800 --> 00:26:27,840
You're giving yourself a minute.

158
00:26:27,840 --> 00:26:29,600
So suppose you sign a contract saying,

159
00:26:29,600 --> 00:26:33,960
if you catch me smoking, I owe you $10,000.

160
00:26:33,960 --> 00:26:36,640
What are you doing about the incentive to increase

161
00:26:36,640 --> 00:26:40,080
your risky behavior if you get the insurance?

162
00:26:40,080 --> 00:26:40,580
Curving it.

163
00:26:40,580 --> 00:26:42,080
Yeah, you're curving that incentive.

164
00:26:42,080 --> 00:26:43,440
Maybe you're getting rid of it.

165
00:26:43,440 --> 00:26:45,760
And what advantage is there for the consumer

166
00:26:45,760 --> 00:26:48,480
of curving your own opportunism?

167
00:26:48,480 --> 00:26:50,240
You're more likely to be sold insurance.

168
00:26:50,240 --> 00:26:52,200
Yes, more likely and lower rates.

169
00:26:52,200 --> 00:26:54,240
Lower rates.

170
00:26:54,240 --> 00:26:56,720
So imagine that you could waive your right to clear bankruptcy

171
00:26:56,720 --> 00:27:00,880
for a loan and then get 1 percentage point off the loan.

172
00:27:00,880 --> 00:27:02,880
That could be a good deal.

173
00:27:02,880 --> 00:27:05,720
So what do contractual punishment schemes for that?

174
00:27:05,720 --> 00:27:07,220
Contractual punishments can be used

175
00:27:07,220 --> 00:27:09,000
for dealing with adverse selection

176
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:12,840
because it is a way for you to convince the seller that you

177
00:27:12,840 --> 00:27:15,800
aren't, that you are, in fact, the good kind.

178
00:27:15,800 --> 00:27:18,520
Says, look, someone who is good would not actually

179
00:27:18,520 --> 00:27:21,680
go and offer me this deal because they

180
00:27:21,680 --> 00:27:23,120
would expect to lose from it.

181
00:27:23,120 --> 00:27:25,560
So the very fact they're offering this deal

182
00:27:25,560 --> 00:27:26,920
means that this is probably going

183
00:27:26,920 --> 00:27:32,280
to be worthwhile to sell the insurance at a low rate.

184
00:27:32,280 --> 00:27:33,740
So yeah, so contractual punishments

185
00:27:33,740 --> 00:27:37,200
can be used for many purposes.

186
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:40,280
So they're used to some minor extent in labor markets,

187
00:27:40,280 --> 00:27:43,480
things like jobs where you have to go and prepay

188
00:27:43,480 --> 00:27:46,640
the cost of your uniform or equipment.

189
00:27:46,640 --> 00:27:48,480
What happens if you go and you get a job

190
00:27:48,480 --> 00:27:51,160
and then you pay for all of your uniform and equipment,

191
00:27:51,160 --> 00:27:53,120
especially stuff you can't resell because it's

192
00:27:53,120 --> 00:27:55,760
the uniform and only of that employer, and then they fire

193
00:27:55,760 --> 00:27:56,640
you?

194
00:27:56,640 --> 00:27:58,280
So suppose you get fired the day that you

195
00:27:58,280 --> 00:28:01,240
start after you shell out a week's worth of income

196
00:28:01,240 --> 00:28:02,880
for this job.

197
00:28:02,880 --> 00:28:06,040
How do you feel about that?

198
00:28:06,040 --> 00:28:07,920
Bad, pretty bad.

199
00:28:07,920 --> 00:28:10,800
So this is a reason for you to behave at least well enough

200
00:28:10,800 --> 00:28:12,200
to not be fired on your first day.

201
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:17,040
Now, my econ one teacher, Bill Dickens,

202
00:28:17,040 --> 00:28:18,920
had a paper co-authored with a bunch of others

203
00:28:18,920 --> 00:28:22,040
about why employers don't use this to a much greater extent.

204
00:28:22,040 --> 00:28:24,560
For example, why is it that in order to get hired,

205
00:28:24,560 --> 00:28:28,480
you don't just post a big bond and have employers then

206
00:28:28,480 --> 00:28:31,920
seize the money if they're disappointed with you?

207
00:28:31,920 --> 00:28:35,440
Now, this is not so different from the old school strategy

208
00:28:35,440 --> 00:28:37,440
of say, I'll work for you for free for a month.

209
00:28:37,440 --> 00:28:39,060
If you don't like me, no hard feelings.

210
00:28:39,060 --> 00:28:41,200
Just fire me.

211
00:28:41,200 --> 00:28:45,520
Now, is that labor contract legal?

212
00:28:45,520 --> 00:28:46,520
Maybe.

213
00:28:46,520 --> 00:28:48,880
It's a definite maybe on its legality.

214
00:28:48,880 --> 00:28:51,880
So isn't zero less than minimum wage?

215
00:28:51,880 --> 00:28:52,920
Yes, it is.

216
00:28:52,920 --> 00:28:56,680
But there is an exception made for unpaid internships.

217
00:28:56,680 --> 00:28:59,400
And then as to whether you could convincingly

218
00:28:59,400 --> 00:29:02,080
argue to a jury that it was, in fact, the case,

219
00:29:02,080 --> 00:29:04,440
or if you could just stop it from ever getting to a jury,

220
00:29:04,440 --> 00:29:06,440
which is another great strategy.

221
00:29:06,440 --> 00:29:08,320
I suppose if it ever got to a jury,

222
00:29:08,320 --> 00:29:10,680
you would lose, but it never gets to a jury.

223
00:29:10,680 --> 00:29:14,640
That's just as good as being in the right, isn't it?

224
00:29:14,640 --> 00:29:15,140
All right.

225
00:29:15,140 --> 00:29:16,240
You're into it.

226
00:29:16,240 --> 00:29:18,320
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

227
00:29:18,320 --> 00:29:18,820
All right.

228
00:29:18,820 --> 00:29:22,360
Yes, so by the way, so the Bureau of Labor Statistics

229
00:29:22,360 --> 00:29:27,200
has official rules for when unpaid internships are legal.

230
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:29,200
And the official standard is so bizarre.

231
00:29:29,200 --> 00:29:31,400
It's almost hard even to say it with a straight face.

232
00:29:31,400 --> 00:29:32,560
But here's the standard.

233
00:29:32,560 --> 00:29:35,160
You are allowed to hire unpaid interns

234
00:29:35,160 --> 00:29:37,640
as long as the interns provide no immediate benefit

235
00:29:37,640 --> 00:29:40,360
to the employer whatsoever.

236
00:29:40,360 --> 00:29:43,200
That's the only way in the case of which it is legal.

237
00:29:43,200 --> 00:29:46,960
So on my reading, if you ever get your boss a cup of coffee

238
00:29:46,960 --> 00:29:51,320
as an intern, then they have to pay minimum wage.

239
00:29:51,320 --> 00:29:54,460
Now, yet unpaid internships continue.

240
00:29:54,460 --> 00:29:55,620
They're all over the place.

241
00:29:55,620 --> 00:29:57,380
And plenty of cups of coffee are probably

242
00:29:57,380 --> 00:29:59,880
brought to bosses without anything happening.

243
00:29:59,880 --> 00:30:01,880
How can this be in the real world?

244
00:30:01,880 --> 00:30:05,960
How can it be that the underpaid internships are existing

245
00:30:05,960 --> 00:30:10,120
in defiance of the actual regulations,

246
00:30:10,120 --> 00:30:13,600
at least the plain language reading of the regulations?

247
00:30:13,600 --> 00:30:17,360
Well, how do the laws get enforced in generally America?

248
00:30:17,360 --> 00:30:18,640
So are you complaining?

249
00:30:18,640 --> 00:30:19,640
Yes, someone complains.

250
00:30:19,640 --> 00:30:22,920
So you'd have to have an intern that comes and sues you.

251
00:30:22,920 --> 00:30:26,040
And this does not sound like a really winning lawsuit, does it?

252
00:30:26,040 --> 00:30:28,640
Because if they only were to intern for you

253
00:30:28,640 --> 00:30:32,440
for free for a summer, then what would you even owe them?

254
00:30:32,440 --> 00:30:33,920
Minimum wage for a summer?

255
00:30:33,920 --> 00:30:36,640
And they've got to get a lawyer for that?

256
00:30:36,640 --> 00:30:38,400
So how much is the lawyer going to charge

257
00:30:38,400 --> 00:30:40,320
to go in and do the case?

258
00:30:40,320 --> 00:30:42,880
Maybe more than the money.

259
00:30:42,880 --> 00:30:44,600
And let's see.

260
00:30:44,600 --> 00:30:46,400
And then on top of it, of course, you

261
00:30:46,400 --> 00:30:48,480
cannot or are in no way sure of winning.

262
00:30:48,480 --> 00:30:50,480
And then there's another little problem,

263
00:30:50,480 --> 00:30:53,000
which is that you might be burning

264
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:56,440
a bunch of bridges in an industry where you want to work.

265
00:30:56,440 --> 00:30:59,800
So even if your employer does not hire you back

266
00:30:59,800 --> 00:31:02,720
for a real job after you do the underpaid internship,

267
00:31:02,720 --> 00:31:06,560
do you want to have them as an enemy?

268
00:31:06,560 --> 00:31:09,360
Is it a good idea to collect enemies in this world?

269
00:31:09,360 --> 00:31:11,360
Would you just recommend that you go around

270
00:31:11,360 --> 00:31:13,240
looking for enemies?

271
00:31:13,240 --> 00:31:14,800
So if you're a professor, you might

272
00:31:14,800 --> 00:31:17,440
want to collect some friendly intellectual adversaries.

273
00:31:17,440 --> 00:31:20,080
That's not quite the same as enemies.

274
00:31:20,080 --> 00:31:24,000
So that's the best story probably for what's going on,

275
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:27,760
is that even though the law is being broken with impunity

276
00:31:27,760 --> 00:31:30,120
all the time, nevertheless, it actually

277
00:31:30,120 --> 00:31:32,520
is almost no one's interest to go pursue these cases.

278
00:31:32,520 --> 00:31:34,840
Really, the only way that it would make sense for these

279
00:31:34,840 --> 00:31:36,680
cases to happen would be if one lawyer were

280
00:31:36,680 --> 00:31:38,720
to do a class action suit where he gets

281
00:31:38,720 --> 00:31:41,120
100 disgruntled interns.

282
00:31:41,120 --> 00:31:42,800
But they all got to be the same firms.

283
00:31:42,800 --> 00:31:44,160
You can't go and sue an industry.

284
00:31:44,160 --> 00:31:46,440
You can't sue 10 different firms when you've

285
00:31:46,440 --> 00:31:47,960
got to go and sue them individually.

286
00:31:47,960 --> 00:31:51,760
So that's, again, a saving grace.

287
00:31:51,760 --> 00:31:55,040
So you've got limiting contractual punishment.

288
00:31:55,040 --> 00:31:57,160
Something else, so there's limiting risk-adjusted

289
00:31:57,160 --> 00:31:58,080
premiums.

290
00:31:58,080 --> 00:32:00,360
So again, the whole idea of adverse selection insurance

291
00:32:00,360 --> 00:32:06,360
is that it is hard for the first people to drop out

292
00:32:06,360 --> 00:32:07,480
are the safe people.

293
00:32:07,480 --> 00:32:09,640
And so the people that stay around and buy insurance

294
00:32:09,640 --> 00:32:12,280
are the high-risk cases.

295
00:32:12,280 --> 00:32:13,960
But this would not happen if firms

296
00:32:13,960 --> 00:32:18,320
could charge different rates based upon different risks.

297
00:32:18,320 --> 00:32:20,640
Now, in a simple adverse selection model,

298
00:32:20,640 --> 00:32:25,440
why don't firms just go and tailor the rates to the risks?

299
00:32:25,440 --> 00:32:27,400
In a simple adverse selection model, why don't?

300
00:32:27,400 --> 00:32:27,680
What's that?

301
00:32:27,680 --> 00:32:28,320
Because they don't know the brand.

302
00:32:28,320 --> 00:32:29,020
They don't know.

303
00:32:29,020 --> 00:32:30,760
The insurance company, despite all

304
00:32:30,760 --> 00:32:33,760
of its quantitative commuting power,

305
00:32:33,760 --> 00:32:36,640
has no clue at all who dies and who lives.

306
00:32:39,200 --> 00:32:41,680
In the real world, of course, insurance companies generally

307
00:32:41,680 --> 00:32:45,280
have great quantitative people who know lots about this stuff.

308
00:32:45,280 --> 00:32:46,640
So if they were unregulated, they

309
00:32:46,640 --> 00:32:50,080
would go and tailor the rates to the risks, which

310
00:32:50,080 --> 00:32:52,440
would then mean that healthy people would

311
00:32:52,440 --> 00:32:55,280
get insurance for low rates.

312
00:32:55,280 --> 00:32:58,400
And what would happen to sick people?

313
00:32:58,400 --> 00:33:03,040
They could still get insurance if they wanted for high rates.

314
00:33:03,040 --> 00:33:05,800
And normally, regulation tries to stop this.

315
00:33:05,800 --> 00:33:07,720
And not entirely, so usually it's

316
00:33:07,720 --> 00:33:10,760
OK to have a little bit of separation.

317
00:33:10,760 --> 00:33:13,720
But regulators don't like it if, say,

318
00:33:13,720 --> 00:33:15,960
a perfectly healthy 18-year-old can get insurance

319
00:33:15,960 --> 00:33:21,760
for $5 a month, and a 60-year-old with cancer

320
00:33:21,760 --> 00:33:26,520
is paying $10,000 premiums.

321
00:33:26,520 --> 00:33:29,600
So then this is why there are regulations that go and try

322
00:33:29,600 --> 00:33:31,280
to compress the premiums.

323
00:33:31,280 --> 00:33:33,680
And a side effect of compressing the premiums

324
00:33:33,680 --> 00:33:36,280
is to cause which market failure?

325
00:33:36,280 --> 00:33:37,040
Adverse selection.

326
00:33:37,040 --> 00:33:39,960
Adverse selection.

327
00:33:39,960 --> 00:33:43,240
And again, if you were to go and push people on this,

328
00:33:43,240 --> 00:33:45,040
I think it's fair to say a lot of people

329
00:33:45,040 --> 00:33:47,760
just have no clue what they're doing.

330
00:33:47,760 --> 00:33:49,800
And adverse selection, in their minds,

331
00:33:49,800 --> 00:33:52,560
is really just a code word for what it is they really

332
00:33:52,560 --> 00:33:57,160
want to do, which is to have low-risk people subsidize

333
00:33:57,160 --> 00:33:59,120
high-risk people.

334
00:33:59,120 --> 00:34:00,880
But again, if people were being more honest,

335
00:34:00,880 --> 00:34:02,360
they would say, well, sure.

336
00:34:02,360 --> 00:34:04,520
There's no market failure at all.

337
00:34:04,520 --> 00:34:06,720
Maybe insurance companies are great at figuring out

338
00:34:06,720 --> 00:34:08,840
who's sick and who's healthy and charging everyone

339
00:34:08,840 --> 00:34:13,600
an actually fair premium plus operating expenses.

340
00:34:13,600 --> 00:34:15,800
But I don't like that outcome.

341
00:34:15,800 --> 00:34:17,480
I don't want an outcome that's efficient.

342
00:34:17,480 --> 00:34:19,040
I want an outcome that's fair.

343
00:34:19,040 --> 00:34:21,520
And a fair outcome is for the healthy people

344
00:34:21,520 --> 00:34:22,600
to subsidize sick people.

345
00:34:25,440 --> 00:34:28,000
Now, again, of course, if this is a case where the sick people

346
00:34:28,000 --> 00:34:30,240
are little children with leukemia,

347
00:34:30,240 --> 00:34:32,440
it's very easy to understand the appeal.

348
00:34:32,440 --> 00:34:36,000
When, as in a lot of states, the high-risk people

349
00:34:36,000 --> 00:34:39,000
are people who have gotten into multiple bad accidents

350
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:41,160
and are still on the roads, then it's

351
00:34:41,160 --> 00:34:43,840
very hard to understand what people are thinking.

352
00:34:43,840 --> 00:34:47,320
Other than my dad's very well-thought-out position

353
00:34:47,320 --> 00:34:50,640
of, Brian, people need to drive.

354
00:34:50,640 --> 00:34:51,800
People need to drive.

355
00:34:51,800 --> 00:34:54,920
It is a requirement of human life to drive.

356
00:34:54,920 --> 00:34:58,600
And if insurance companies can charge really high-risk people

357
00:34:58,600 --> 00:35:02,360
really high rates, they won't be able to afford driving.

358
00:35:02,360 --> 00:35:04,240
An approved version of this would be, well,

359
00:35:04,240 --> 00:35:05,320
those people drive anyway.

360
00:35:05,320 --> 00:35:07,280
But then it's like, well, you don't

361
00:35:07,280 --> 00:35:10,360
take that excuse from people who lose their licenses altogether.

362
00:35:10,360 --> 00:35:17,080
So why take that excuse from people who could have gotten,

363
00:35:17,080 --> 00:35:19,520
were legal to drive, but they drove without insurance

364
00:35:19,520 --> 00:35:22,000
even though they were really high-risk?

365
00:35:22,000 --> 00:35:23,120
So anyway, you got that one.

366
00:35:23,120 --> 00:35:26,400
And then last one that I have a whole book on

367
00:35:26,400 --> 00:35:29,600
is subsidizing education.

368
00:35:29,600 --> 00:35:32,280
So if you believe that there's much to the signaling

369
00:35:32,280 --> 00:35:34,320
model of education, this means that there

370
00:35:34,320 --> 00:35:36,280
is an inefficiently high level of education

371
00:35:36,280 --> 00:35:38,920
because one of the main reasons to go to school

372
00:35:38,920 --> 00:35:41,440
is to show off and impress employers.

373
00:35:41,440 --> 00:35:44,640
But if everyone shows off, if everyone impresses employers,

374
00:35:44,640 --> 00:35:46,280
this just raises the expectations

375
00:35:46,280 --> 00:35:48,080
for how much education you have to have,

376
00:35:48,080 --> 00:35:51,000
causing credential inflation, where

377
00:35:51,000 --> 00:35:53,040
the amount of education that you need

378
00:35:53,040 --> 00:35:54,880
is higher than for one and the same job

379
00:35:54,880 --> 00:35:57,800
or is higher than the past.

380
00:35:57,800 --> 00:35:59,560
So economist Richard Vedder has this joke

381
00:35:59,560 --> 00:36:02,120
about a future where to get a job as a janitor,

382
00:36:02,120 --> 00:36:04,640
you need a master's in janitorial science.

383
00:36:04,640 --> 00:36:06,520
A reader from Korea actually sent me

384
00:36:06,520 --> 00:36:08,920
what appears to be a genuine news article

385
00:36:08,920 --> 00:36:12,320
about how a third of the applicants

386
00:36:12,320 --> 00:36:15,680
for a government janitorial job in South Korea

387
00:36:15,680 --> 00:36:18,280
were college graduates.

388
00:36:18,280 --> 00:36:20,520
And now the people hired three have bachelor's degrees

389
00:36:20,520 --> 00:36:23,320
and one had an associate's degree.

390
00:36:23,320 --> 00:36:26,840
So it's not actually a master's in janitorial science,

391
00:36:26,840 --> 00:36:28,960
just a bachelor's degree in janitorial science.

392
00:36:28,960 --> 00:36:30,360
Or of course, they don't really have

393
00:36:30,360 --> 00:36:31,720
bachelor's degrees in janitorial science.

394
00:36:31,720 --> 00:36:34,000
They probably have a bachelor's degree

395
00:36:34,000 --> 00:36:35,760
in, say, Korean literature.

396
00:36:35,760 --> 00:36:38,520
And then they wind up competing very fiercely

397
00:36:38,520 --> 00:36:41,160
to get a job as a janitor.

398
00:36:41,160 --> 00:36:43,040
Great system, right?

399
00:36:43,040 --> 00:36:46,000
Sounds like a path to a successful society

400
00:36:46,000 --> 00:36:49,160
is to have all of your janitors spending 17 years in school.

401
00:36:52,040 --> 00:36:56,080
So that is another example of what I'm talking about,

402
00:36:56,080 --> 00:36:58,040
where in a single signaling model,

403
00:36:58,040 --> 00:37:00,040
there's already a general tendency

404
00:37:00,040 --> 00:37:01,560
to excessive education.

405
00:37:01,560 --> 00:37:03,960
But then what does government come along and do?

406
00:37:03,960 --> 00:37:06,280
Subsidize it to make the problem worse,

407
00:37:06,280 --> 00:37:08,760
which is also known as pouring gasoline on the fire.

408
00:37:11,440 --> 00:37:12,920
So questions about any of this?

409
00:37:16,560 --> 00:37:20,720
All this means you can often admit

410
00:37:20,720 --> 00:37:23,320
that a severe market failure exists

411
00:37:23,320 --> 00:37:27,480
and then still blame the government for making it worse.

412
00:37:27,480 --> 00:37:30,080
Because the fact that government could improve the situation

413
00:37:30,080 --> 00:37:31,400
does not mean that it does.

414
00:37:31,400 --> 00:37:32,560
You've got to look at the world and see

415
00:37:32,560 --> 00:37:34,080
what government is really doing.

416
00:37:34,080 --> 00:37:36,200
And just like in the old Soviet Union,

417
00:37:36,200 --> 00:37:38,400
there was an enormous amount of pollution.

418
00:37:38,400 --> 00:37:39,960
There were socialists who said, look,

419
00:37:39,960 --> 00:37:41,840
as long as government is running the economy,

420
00:37:41,840 --> 00:37:44,920
then they will optimally trade off the cost and benefits

421
00:37:44,920 --> 00:37:47,240
pollution so there won't be any pollution problem.

422
00:37:47,240 --> 00:37:49,680
But the Soviet Union seemed to have a horrible problem.

423
00:37:49,680 --> 00:37:52,080
And it looked like even though the government was running

424
00:37:52,080 --> 00:37:53,800
the industries, they seemed more focused

425
00:37:53,800 --> 00:37:56,840
on making a lot of tanks this year than about cancer

426
00:37:56,840 --> 00:37:57,640
10 years from now.

427
00:37:57,640 --> 00:38:00,120
All right.

428
00:38:00,120 --> 00:38:03,040
OK, so everyone happy?

429
00:38:03,040 --> 00:38:04,920
Or suitably peeved?

430
00:38:04,920 --> 00:38:07,880
Or inspired to go and carry out a great research

431
00:38:07,880 --> 00:38:10,760
program to expose the horrors of the system?

432
00:38:10,760 --> 00:38:13,840
All right, there's all sorts of possible reactions.

433
00:38:13,840 --> 00:38:16,360
All right, now let's move on.

434
00:38:16,360 --> 00:38:18,240
So up till now, I've occasionally

435
00:38:18,240 --> 00:38:20,200
alluded to the existence of another field called

436
00:38:20,200 --> 00:38:22,880
psychology.

437
00:38:22,880 --> 00:38:26,040
Now, when I was in grad school, the word psychology

438
00:38:26,040 --> 00:38:29,600
was never pronounced without a sneer or sarcasm.

439
00:38:29,600 --> 00:38:31,800
Psych-a-ly-ty.

440
00:38:31,800 --> 00:38:35,880
If you just want to be psychologists about this.

441
00:38:35,880 --> 00:38:38,640
All right, anyway, I'm not going to do this

442
00:38:38,640 --> 00:38:40,840
because there is so much that economists

443
00:38:40,840 --> 00:38:42,000
have learned from psychology.

444
00:38:42,000 --> 00:38:43,360
There's so much we have learned.

445
00:38:43,360 --> 00:38:45,240
But even better, there's still a lot of stuff

446
00:38:45,240 --> 00:38:46,960
that hardly any economists are aware of.

447
00:38:46,960 --> 00:38:48,680
And all you need to do to get papers

448
00:38:48,680 --> 00:38:53,040
is to find ways of finding out what is so much

449
00:38:53,040 --> 00:38:54,960
something useful psychologists know

450
00:38:54,960 --> 00:38:56,920
and then be the first economist to apply it

451
00:38:56,920 --> 00:38:58,460
to something an economist care about.

452
00:38:58,460 --> 00:39:00,480
And then you've got a paper.

453
00:39:00,480 --> 00:39:02,680
Now, of course, we're going to be talking about are mostly

454
00:39:02,680 --> 00:39:04,140
cases where it's already been done.

455
00:39:04,140 --> 00:39:06,480
But there's so much more out there

456
00:39:06,480 --> 00:39:09,480
because psychology is an incredibly

457
00:39:09,480 --> 00:39:11,560
diverse and broad field.

458
00:39:11,560 --> 00:39:13,040
There are many kinds of psychologists

459
00:39:13,040 --> 00:39:15,120
that don't even talk to each other.

460
00:39:15,120 --> 00:39:17,320
So this means that you could just

461
00:39:17,320 --> 00:39:19,120
go and talk to a bunch of different kinds

462
00:39:19,120 --> 00:39:24,000
and likely to go and find something out that you can use.

463
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:25,000
So let's get started.

464
00:39:28,600 --> 00:39:32,120
Now, most economists just take the basic assumptions

465
00:39:32,120 --> 00:39:35,240
of microeconomics for granted.

466
00:39:35,240 --> 00:39:35,920
That's there.

467
00:39:35,920 --> 00:39:38,080
That's what we know.

468
00:39:38,080 --> 00:39:40,240
So very rarely do economists empirically

469
00:39:40,240 --> 00:39:42,840
test the basic microassumptions.

470
00:39:42,840 --> 00:39:47,120
But you might say, well, the elementary micro theory

471
00:39:47,120 --> 00:39:49,320
almost seems true by definition.

472
00:39:49,320 --> 00:39:52,640
Like the thing where the transitivity,

473
00:39:52,640 --> 00:39:57,520
like if A is preferred to B, B preferred to C,

474
00:39:57,520 --> 00:40:24,880
then A is going to be preferred to C.


1
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:01,600
You've seen that one before?

2
00:40:01,600 --> 00:40:04,040
All right, so isn't that, at least that kind of just seems

3
00:40:04,040 --> 00:40:04,800
true by definition.

4
00:40:04,800 --> 00:40:07,400
How could anything be otherwise?

5
00:40:07,400 --> 00:40:07,900
All right.

6
00:40:11,120 --> 00:40:13,720
But we're actually going to see even that stuff you can test.

7
00:40:13,720 --> 00:40:17,200
But in any case, if we assume the preferences are stable,

8
00:40:17,200 --> 00:40:18,780
preferences are stable, they don't just

9
00:40:18,780 --> 00:40:20,840
jump around all the time.

10
00:40:20,840 --> 00:40:23,440
And this is almost always done in empirical work.

11
00:40:23,440 --> 00:40:24,840
When economists do empirical work,

12
00:40:24,840 --> 00:40:27,920
they almost always assume stable preferences.

13
00:40:27,920 --> 00:40:31,040
In that case, we get a lot of testable assumptions.

14
00:40:31,040 --> 00:40:34,280
Why would economists assume stable preferences?

15
00:40:34,280 --> 00:40:43,840
Suppose that the supply goes up and the price goes up.

16
00:40:43,840 --> 00:40:47,560
And then you were just to say, well, what must have happened

17
00:40:47,560 --> 00:40:51,600
was that the preferences of consumers changed.

18
00:40:51,600 --> 00:40:55,240
And so demand went up and went off at the same time, too.

19
00:40:55,240 --> 00:40:56,840
We have no actual evidence of this.

20
00:40:56,840 --> 00:40:58,520
You just say it.

21
00:40:58,520 --> 00:41:00,860
This would not seem very satisfying to most economists,

22
00:41:00,860 --> 00:41:02,960
because it seems like it's a theory that

23
00:41:02,960 --> 00:41:05,720
explains whatever happens.

24
00:41:05,720 --> 00:41:09,680
And theories that explain all conceivable events,

25
00:41:09,680 --> 00:41:10,960
what do we call those?

26
00:41:10,960 --> 00:41:11,840
Unfalse survival.

27
00:41:11,840 --> 00:41:13,720
Unfalse survival, or tautologies,

28
00:41:13,720 --> 00:41:16,880
or crummy theories.

29
00:41:16,880 --> 00:41:18,240
It's like, wait a second.

30
00:41:18,240 --> 00:41:20,200
If capitalism does well, then you

31
00:41:20,200 --> 00:41:21,920
say that it's exploiting the third world.

32
00:41:21,920 --> 00:41:23,360
And as poorly, you say that it's collapsing

33
00:41:23,360 --> 00:41:25,080
under its internal congregations.

34
00:41:25,080 --> 00:41:28,200
So what are the observations that shows that you're wrong?

35
00:41:28,200 --> 00:41:30,760
There are no observations that show I'm wrong,

36
00:41:30,760 --> 00:41:32,920
because I'm right.

37
00:41:32,920 --> 00:41:34,680
There is a little bit of pushback to this.

38
00:41:34,680 --> 00:41:37,920
So Fisher Black had the line, the easiest theory

39
00:41:37,920 --> 00:41:41,000
to falsify is one that's false.

40
00:41:41,000 --> 00:41:45,200
But still, there is a lot of resistance

41
00:41:45,200 --> 00:41:47,840
to unfalsifiable theories.

42
00:41:47,840 --> 00:41:50,320
And then furthermore, if we go on

43
00:41:50,320 --> 00:41:52,080
and assume that preferences are selfish

44
00:41:52,080 --> 00:41:55,160
in the ordinary language sense of the word,

45
00:41:55,160 --> 00:41:57,680
and this is also a very common auxiliary assumption

46
00:41:57,680 --> 00:41:59,560
for economists to use.

47
00:41:59,560 --> 00:42:03,360
In like, if demand for wheat goes up,

48
00:42:03,360 --> 00:42:06,120
we normally think that customers are going to pay more.

49
00:42:06,120 --> 00:42:08,200
But imagine someone would say, well,

50
00:42:08,200 --> 00:42:12,040
but since wheat farmers love their fellow men,

51
00:42:12,040 --> 00:42:14,240
when they find out that other people need more food,

52
00:42:14,240 --> 00:42:16,320
they'll work harder to go and grow them more food.

53
00:42:16,320 --> 00:42:20,120
And so maybe the price won't go up at all.

54
00:42:20,120 --> 00:42:25,120
And in terms of logical possibility, is that wrong?

55
00:42:25,120 --> 00:42:29,000
Yeah, you know, imagine that the consumer is my son,

56
00:42:29,000 --> 00:42:30,400
and I'm the farm owner.

57
00:42:30,400 --> 00:42:31,880
And then my son wants more food.

58
00:42:31,880 --> 00:42:33,840
Do I go and check out the price for my kid?

59
00:42:33,840 --> 00:42:36,800
So now for now, dinner costs $10, kid.

60
00:42:36,800 --> 00:42:38,640
No, normally if my kids get hungrier,

61
00:42:38,640 --> 00:42:40,880
I just get more food and give it to them.

62
00:42:40,880 --> 00:42:46,040
So that's what it's like to have a market where the sellers love

63
00:42:46,040 --> 00:42:51,440
the buyers so much that the normal functioning doesn't

64
00:42:51,440 --> 00:42:53,080
really hold anymore.

65
00:42:53,080 --> 00:42:56,160
So lurking in the background of our story about markets

66
00:42:56,160 --> 00:42:58,720
is the very plausible assumption that wheat farmers don't

67
00:42:58,720 --> 00:43:04,160
love you, and that they're just growing wheat for the money.

68
00:43:04,160 --> 00:43:06,040
Can you believe this?

69
00:43:06,040 --> 00:43:08,560
You ever thought about how little the wheat farmer loves

70
00:43:08,560 --> 00:43:09,200
you?

71
00:43:09,200 --> 00:43:10,720
You're going to get a bag of flour.

72
00:43:10,720 --> 00:43:11,880
And so he's going to buy it.

73
00:43:11,880 --> 00:43:13,080
And I thought he loved me.

74
00:43:13,080 --> 00:43:15,000
But now, when I just realized he's just

75
00:43:15,000 --> 00:43:17,200
selling me flour for money, for what?

76
00:43:20,000 --> 00:43:21,880
And then when we expand things further

77
00:43:21,880 --> 00:43:25,520
to expected utility theory, expected utility theory

78
00:43:25,520 --> 00:43:28,960
adds on a bunch of further empirical implications.

79
00:43:28,960 --> 00:43:35,080
Things like there will be a linear effect of a risk going

80
00:43:35,080 --> 00:43:38,280
from 0 to 1, or 1 to 0, or 1 to 2.

81
00:43:38,280 --> 00:43:40,520
So remember, one implication of expected utility theory

82
00:43:40,520 --> 00:43:42,720
is people won't pay a special premium

83
00:43:42,720 --> 00:43:45,160
to drive risk down to 0.

84
00:43:45,160 --> 00:43:48,200
They'll pay the same amount to go from 2 to 1

85
00:43:48,200 --> 00:43:50,160
as they'll go from 1 to 0.

86
00:43:50,160 --> 00:43:51,040
And that's testable.

87
00:43:51,040 --> 00:43:54,360
And you probably have an intuition that's wrong, right?

88
00:43:54,360 --> 00:43:57,240
And that intuition is very likely very true.

89
00:44:00,040 --> 00:44:03,320
So there's a big literature at this point, which strangely

90
00:44:03,320 --> 00:44:06,280
is usually called behavioral economics, which

91
00:44:06,280 --> 00:44:10,120
does all the tests that other economists,

92
00:44:10,120 --> 00:44:12,800
both economists usually don't bother doing.

93
00:44:12,800 --> 00:44:14,560
Again, why behavioral economics?

94
00:44:14,560 --> 00:44:15,720
What kind of a name is that?

95
00:44:15,720 --> 00:44:16,880
It's a stupid name, right?

96
00:44:16,880 --> 00:44:18,880
It's like, well, all economics is about behavior.

97
00:44:18,880 --> 00:44:20,920
So what are you talking?

98
00:44:20,920 --> 00:44:23,080
Anyway, this is another one of those cases

99
00:44:23,080 --> 00:44:27,440
where you would either fight an entire profession for a word,

100
00:44:27,440 --> 00:44:30,440
or you can just get with the program and use the same word

101
00:44:30,440 --> 00:44:31,840
in the same way as everyone else,

102
00:44:31,840 --> 00:44:33,200
and everyone understands you.

103
00:44:33,200 --> 00:44:34,880
And then the only drawback is when you teach it

104
00:44:34,880 --> 00:44:38,640
to someone else, you have to give this thing up.

105
00:44:38,640 --> 00:44:40,400
But again, it would have been much better

106
00:44:40,400 --> 00:44:42,960
if it had been called psychological economics.

107
00:44:42,960 --> 00:44:46,120
Or sometimes it is just called psychology and economics,

108
00:44:46,120 --> 00:44:47,160
something like that.

109
00:44:47,160 --> 00:44:49,080
Or it could have been called that.

110
00:44:49,080 --> 00:44:50,400
And those are alternate ones.

111
00:44:50,400 --> 00:44:53,560
But behavioral econ is the name that it's primarily used.

112
00:44:53,560 --> 00:44:55,920
So that's the one we're going to be talking about.

113
00:44:55,920 --> 00:44:59,480
It's basically psychologically informed economics.

114
00:45:02,560 --> 00:45:05,920
So behavioral economics goes and does empirical tests

115
00:45:05,920 --> 00:45:08,000
that most economists just don't bother doing,

116
00:45:08,000 --> 00:45:10,920
because they think they know how they're going to turn out.

117
00:45:10,920 --> 00:45:13,680
Or they just think of them as so true by definition,

118
00:45:13,680 --> 00:45:15,760
how could they be wrong?

119
00:45:15,760 --> 00:45:18,560
And the product of behavioral economics

120
00:45:18,560 --> 00:45:23,200
is anomalies, results that are wrong.

121
00:45:23,200 --> 00:45:26,040
Or namely, things that are the empirical results that

122
00:45:26,040 --> 00:45:28,600
seem to congregate very simple economic models.

123
00:45:31,120 --> 00:45:33,240
And of course, one thing you can do

124
00:45:33,240 --> 00:45:34,720
is try to make the anomaly go away

125
00:45:34,720 --> 00:45:36,360
and say, well, it looks like anomaly actually

126
00:45:36,360 --> 00:45:37,120
really makes sense.

127
00:45:37,120 --> 00:45:40,080
It can be explained totally by normal micro theory.

128
00:45:40,080 --> 00:45:42,920
Another thing you can do is, no, the facts are solid.

129
00:45:42,920 --> 00:45:44,200
The theory's wrong.

130
00:45:44,200 --> 00:45:47,240
You can do that too.

131
00:45:47,240 --> 00:45:48,880
And by the way, who is the economist

132
00:45:48,880 --> 00:45:51,080
who famously had a column in the Journal of Economics

133
00:45:51,080 --> 00:45:52,600
called Anomalies?

134
00:45:52,600 --> 00:45:54,760
Richard Naylor.

135
00:45:54,760 --> 00:45:56,440
And at least some people are suggesting

136
00:45:56,440 --> 00:45:59,600
he's the only economist to ever get a Nobel Prize for a column.

137
00:45:59,600 --> 00:46:05,920
I think that is, there's a chance that's right, actually.

138
00:46:05,920 --> 00:46:07,400
So what you normally would do with the columns

139
00:46:07,400 --> 00:46:09,000
is you talk about his own research

140
00:46:09,000 --> 00:46:10,200
and research of other people.

141
00:46:10,200 --> 00:46:12,160
Sometimes it would only be about other people

142
00:46:12,160 --> 00:46:13,280
if he hadn't been involved.

143
00:46:13,280 --> 00:46:15,480
But he did have his finger in, say, many pies.

144
00:46:15,480 --> 00:46:17,400
And so if you could go and mention that he'd

145
00:46:17,400 --> 00:46:19,400
