00:51:26,040 --> 00:51:28,120
Or the farmer was wrong.

263
00:51:28,120 --> 00:51:29,480
Did he?

264
00:51:29,480 --> 00:51:32,520
Or did he just round according to normal conventions

265
00:51:32,520 --> 00:51:33,960
and what he told you is, in fact,

266
00:51:33,960 --> 00:51:38,480
exactly true to the normal number of decimal places?

267
00:51:38,480 --> 00:51:42,000
Anyway, to my mind, that's one of the really odd things

268
00:51:42,000 --> 00:51:44,400
when people claim that all theories are false is,

269
00:51:44,400 --> 00:51:47,400
to an infinite number of decimal places, fine, but who cares?

270
00:51:47,400 --> 00:51:48,360
But are they false?

271
00:51:48,360 --> 00:51:50,960
But are some theories false to a practical number

272
00:51:50,960 --> 00:51:51,880
of decimal places?

273
00:51:51,880 --> 00:51:55,000
That's good enough.

274
00:51:55,000 --> 00:51:58,440
But anyway, when someone says it doesn't matter whether or not

275
00:51:58,440 --> 00:52:02,000
that turns out theory is wrong, it matters prediction,

276
00:52:02,000 --> 00:52:03,800
main thing in behavioral economics is, fine,

277
00:52:03,800 --> 00:52:06,440
we show that our predictions are wrong.

278
00:52:06,440 --> 00:52:10,280
So don't stop listening.

279
00:52:10,280 --> 00:52:11,160
Just look on.

280
00:52:11,160 --> 00:52:13,160
You want to find out about wrong predictions?

281
00:52:13,160 --> 00:52:16,840
I'll tell you some wrong predictions.

282
00:52:16,840 --> 00:52:20,480
Second common objection is to say the deviations cancel out.

283
00:52:20,480 --> 00:52:22,560
And again, the standard behavioral econ answer

284
00:52:22,560 --> 00:52:26,160
is not the ones I'm telling you about anyway.

285
00:52:26,160 --> 00:52:29,560
In fact, I've got a bunch where they don't cancel out.

286
00:52:29,560 --> 00:52:31,520
So these are systematic differences,

287
00:52:31,520 --> 00:52:33,040
systematic oddities.

288
00:52:33,040 --> 00:52:36,520
In things like, if some people overpay

289
00:52:36,520 --> 00:52:39,120
to reduce the risk to zero and some underpay,

290
00:52:39,120 --> 00:52:40,480
then it might bounce out.

291
00:52:40,480 --> 00:52:42,440
What if there's just a systematic human tendency

292
00:52:42,440 --> 00:52:46,840
to overpay for getting risk down to zero?

293
00:52:46,840 --> 00:52:49,400
And then a third common objection,

294
00:52:49,400 --> 00:52:51,080
this is one which I have more sympathy,

295
00:52:51,080 --> 00:52:52,520
but I'll just run with it anyway.

296
00:52:52,520 --> 00:52:55,320
It's to say the anomalies rise to a mean sentence.

297
00:52:55,320 --> 00:52:57,440
And at least one common reply is to say

298
00:52:57,440 --> 00:53:00,920
that actually the stronger incentives often don't matter.

299
00:53:00,920 --> 00:53:03,520
And many of the anomalies have been seen in financial markets

300
00:53:03,520 --> 00:53:07,800
where it seems like there's a big incentive to get it right.

301
00:53:07,800 --> 00:53:12,160
If you follow Nassim Taleb and Richard Thaler on Twitter,

302
00:53:12,160 --> 00:53:15,080
recently Richard Thaler was scolding Nassim Taleb

303
00:53:15,080 --> 00:53:17,720
for his skin in the game slogan and saying,

304
00:53:17,720 --> 00:53:19,220
actually, there's not much evidence

305
00:53:19,220 --> 00:53:21,680
that skin in the game matters.

306
00:53:21,680 --> 00:53:24,880
If you listen to the Dan Cahan seminar that

307
00:53:24,880 --> 00:53:28,040
happened at the beginning of the semester,

308
00:53:28,040 --> 00:53:30,280
he actually presented some really interesting evidence

309
00:53:30,280 --> 00:53:32,120
that incentives matter a lot.

310
00:53:32,120 --> 00:53:35,880
In particular, that making people pay something

311
00:53:35,880 --> 00:53:37,800
when they make a mistake does actually

312
00:53:37,800 --> 00:53:39,840
increase accuracy of predictions.

313
00:53:39,840 --> 00:53:42,320
And particularly showing is that a lot of ideological bias

314
00:53:42,320 --> 00:53:46,120
goes away if you get penalized for wrong answers.

315
00:53:49,040 --> 00:53:51,240
So anyway, so to some extent over the next two weeks,

316
00:53:51,240 --> 00:53:53,080
I'm going to be playing devil's advocate.

317
00:53:53,080 --> 00:53:55,640
So I may overstate the case when I

318
00:53:55,640 --> 00:53:59,160
am going through the evidence.

319
00:53:59,160 --> 00:54:02,960
What's odd about this stuff is there are a lot of economists

320
00:54:02,960 --> 00:54:05,160
have either one strong reaction or another.

321
00:54:05,160 --> 00:54:07,440
So this reaction of behavioral economics

322
00:54:07,440 --> 00:54:10,880
is the only useful thing to know and everyone else is an idiot.

323
00:54:10,880 --> 00:54:14,560
And anyone who even tries to go and use

324
00:54:14,560 --> 00:54:17,800
a standard neoclassical model to explain something

325
00:54:17,800 --> 00:54:19,480
is just being an automatic fool.

326
00:54:19,480 --> 00:54:21,880
And there's another one saying that behavioral economists

327
00:54:21,880 --> 00:54:23,840
don't know anything and they're just

328
00:54:23,840 --> 00:54:26,840
going to try to ruin a great subject.

329
00:54:26,840 --> 00:54:29,520
And I have heard both these reactions.

330
00:54:29,520 --> 00:54:34,440
So hopefully we will reach a happy medium, a nuanced medium.

331
00:54:34,440 --> 00:54:36,600
You don't need to just say they're half right in every

332
00:54:36,600 --> 00:54:37,100
case.

333
00:54:37,100 --> 00:54:40,200
You can say some cases neoclassical view is right,

334
00:54:40,200 --> 00:54:44,840
some cases behavioral view is right, and so on.

335
00:54:44,840 --> 00:54:46,680
So questions about this?

336
00:54:46,680 --> 00:54:49,240
Everyone happy?

337
00:54:49,240 --> 00:54:51,920
I see that phone is really strangely off.

338
00:54:51,920 --> 00:54:55,000
I guess they just never just stay.

339
00:54:55,000 --> 00:55:00,320
So let's just start off with the case of preference reversals.

340
00:55:00,320 --> 00:55:05,440
So for practical purposes, economists almost always

341
00:55:05,440 --> 00:55:08,960
assume that people have constant preferences over outcome.

342
00:55:08,960 --> 00:55:13,800
Constant preferences over the outcome.

343
00:55:13,800 --> 00:55:15,320
What does outcome mean?

344
00:55:15,320 --> 00:55:17,840
It's basically your list of stuff,

345
00:55:17,840 --> 00:55:19,480
your inventory of possessions.

346
00:55:19,480 --> 00:55:20,760
That's your outcome.

347
00:55:20,760 --> 00:55:22,760
And again, constant preferences mean

348
00:55:22,760 --> 00:55:26,400
that the amount that you like the stuff does not change.

349
00:55:26,400 --> 00:55:28,480
So you just keep liking it over time.

350
00:55:31,760 --> 00:55:33,640
So behavioral economists have a bunch of what

351
00:55:33,640 --> 00:55:35,360
appear to be counter examples.

352
00:55:35,360 --> 00:55:38,840
And you can get an individual to prefer A to B or B to A

353
00:55:38,840 --> 00:55:41,920
for what appear to be completely irrelevant reasons.

354
00:55:41,920 --> 00:55:46,040
And when this occurs, it's called a reference reversal.

355
00:55:46,040 --> 00:55:48,160
So what is a really clean example?

356
00:55:48,160 --> 00:55:50,280
A really clean example is when you

357
00:55:50,280 --> 00:55:52,480
have two logically identical descriptions

358
00:55:52,480 --> 00:55:54,040
of the same situation.

359
00:55:54,040 --> 00:55:56,200
But people prefer one.

360
00:55:56,200 --> 00:55:58,840
They'll prefer one that's described in a certain way

361
00:55:58,840 --> 00:56:00,600
and the other described the other way.

362
00:56:00,600 --> 00:56:01,440
So think about this.

363
00:56:01,440 --> 00:56:03,520
Suppose that you want to convince someone

364
00:56:03,520 --> 00:56:04,240
to get surgery.

365
00:56:04,240 --> 00:56:07,800
You're a crooked doctor, or maybe you just know better.

366
00:56:07,800 --> 00:56:12,280
So suppose that the surgery has a 90% chance of not killing.

367
00:56:12,280 --> 00:56:13,640
10% chance of killing.

368
00:56:13,640 --> 00:56:18,040
So 90% chance of surviving, 10% chance of dying.

369
00:56:24,360 --> 00:56:27,240
90% chance you survive, 10% chance you'll die.

370
00:56:27,240 --> 00:56:31,240
Now notice, if that's true, is that always true?

371
00:56:31,240 --> 00:56:31,760
Yes.

372
00:56:31,760 --> 00:56:34,120
Yes, these are two logically identical ways

373
00:56:34,120 --> 00:56:37,720
of describing the very same distribution of events.

374
00:56:37,720 --> 00:56:40,200
Now, if anyone here ever worked in advertising or marketing

375
00:56:40,200 --> 00:56:43,480
or anything, just common guess, just common sense,

376
00:56:43,480 --> 00:56:45,600
which one do you want to use to sell someone

377
00:56:45,600 --> 00:56:47,280
on your chart, your description, do you

378
00:56:47,280 --> 00:56:50,360
want to use to sell them on your surgery?

379
00:56:50,360 --> 00:56:52,160
90% chance of surviving.

380
00:56:52,160 --> 00:56:53,960
You've got a 90% chance of surviving.

381
00:56:53,960 --> 00:56:56,340
Suppose you want to convince them to not get the surgery.

382
00:56:56,340 --> 00:56:59,080
Then you say, 10% chance of dying.

383
00:56:59,080 --> 00:57:01,320
You're going to die.

384
00:57:01,320 --> 00:57:03,680
These are just two different ways

385
00:57:03,680 --> 00:57:07,640
of organizing words to describe exactly the same events.

386
00:57:07,640 --> 00:57:12,520
So if human beings had conscious preferences over outcomes,

387
00:57:12,520 --> 00:57:14,520
wouldn't make the slightest difference which way

388
00:57:14,520 --> 00:57:15,720
you describe them.

389
00:57:15,720 --> 00:57:17,960
But there are plenty of experiments

390
00:57:17,960 --> 00:57:21,040
where you see that it does matter.

391
00:57:21,040 --> 00:57:23,920
Yes, if you do use this description,

392
00:57:23,920 --> 00:57:27,880
there'll be higher uptake than if you use this description.

393
00:57:27,880 --> 00:57:29,600
Most of the evidence is hypothetical,

394
00:57:29,600 --> 00:57:30,840
like imagine we're patients.

395
00:57:30,840 --> 00:57:32,640
I don't know what you're talking about.

396
00:57:32,640 --> 00:57:34,160
Or actually, I think there's also

397
00:57:34,160 --> 00:57:36,560
ones where they give medical students hypotheticals.

398
00:57:36,560 --> 00:57:38,160
Like, what do you like?

399
00:57:38,160 --> 00:57:41,280
You're randomly assigned to saying the treatment is 90%,

400
00:57:41,280 --> 00:57:43,960
90% survival rate, or 10% death rate.

401
00:57:43,960 --> 00:57:46,840
And even medical professionals, at least aspiring medical

402
00:57:46,840 --> 00:57:49,080
professionals, give pretty different answers

403
00:57:49,080 --> 00:57:52,120
based on the description.

404
00:57:52,120 --> 00:57:54,480
You may even notice that if you've

405
00:57:54,480 --> 00:57:56,520
described yourself as either a glass half empty

406
00:57:56,520 --> 00:57:58,280
or a glass half full kind of person,

407
00:57:58,280 --> 00:58:01,160
notice these are the same thing.

408
00:58:01,160 --> 00:58:04,320
The only difference is how you describe or conceive

409
00:58:04,320 --> 00:58:06,840
