01:13:02,160 --> 01:13:08,160
It's much less common for someone to go and think they want a baby and then say, oh my god, I don't want one after all.

179
01:13:08,160 --> 01:13:15,160
So, again, it seems like just even receiving an embryo is enough to make people say,

180
01:13:15,160 --> 01:13:19,160
you know that embryo I was struggling with the whole night to not have?

181
01:13:19,160 --> 01:13:22,160
Now I would do anything to get him.

182
01:13:22,160 --> 01:13:25,160
That's odd, right?

183
01:13:25,160 --> 01:13:28,160
Okay.

184
01:13:28,160 --> 01:13:34,160
So that is the endowment effect. And why don't we go and take our 10 minute break now.

185
01:13:34,160 --> 01:13:37,160
Thank you.

186
01:13:37,160 --> 01:13:41,160
Alright, so questions about the endowment effect?

187
01:13:41,160 --> 01:13:44,160
We're learning about the endowment effect.

188
01:13:44,160 --> 01:13:56,160
Alright. In that case, we'll move on to something similar, which is status quo bias.

189
01:13:56,160 --> 01:13:58,160
Alright.

190
01:13:58,160 --> 01:14:03,160
So, experimentally, it seems that people are often reluctant to either buy or sell.

191
01:14:03,160 --> 01:14:11,160
So whatever mixture of things you give people, they don't want either, at least there is a reluctance to go and adjust it.

192
01:14:11,160 --> 01:14:15,160
Even if it's an experiment where you just randomly handed out stuff to people,

193
01:14:15,160 --> 01:14:20,160
and you might think that then if anyone happened to be randomly given an unusually large amount of something,

194
01:14:20,160 --> 01:14:27,160
they might not want to sell it, but it seems like people like to just stick with what they've got to a surprising extent.

195
01:14:27,160 --> 01:14:29,160
Alright.

196
01:14:29,160 --> 01:14:31,160
Okay.

197
01:14:31,160 --> 01:14:34,160
And furthermore, you can make something more popular just by calling it the status quo,

198
01:14:34,160 --> 01:14:39,160
just by saying we start here, and then what do you want to do?

199
01:14:39,160 --> 01:14:44,160
When you think about government policy, at least to me, these seem like very egregious cases where

200
01:14:44,160 --> 01:14:48,160
suppose that people just weren't aware that there's foreign language requirements in US schools,

201
01:14:48,160 --> 01:14:50,160
and then you said, you know what we need to do?

202
01:14:50,160 --> 01:14:53,160
We need to add a foreign language requirement.

203
01:14:53,160 --> 01:14:56,160
Probably a lot of people would say, well, I don't know, that's really such a good idea,

204
01:14:56,160 --> 01:14:59,160
and if you really need foreign languages in the modern age, we'd better do computer language.

205
01:14:59,160 --> 01:15:02,160
But if they know that we already have it, you say, well, why don't we get rid of it?

206
01:15:02,160 --> 01:15:08,160
Then it's like, oh no, well then, I mean, there's so many uses for it, it trains the mind, it's so good,

207
01:15:08,160 --> 01:15:14,160
and you see this in foreign policy.

208
01:15:14,160 --> 01:15:18,160
So should we go and support rebels in Syria?

209
01:15:18,160 --> 01:15:21,160
Well, are we supporting rebels in Syria?

210
01:15:21,160 --> 01:15:25,160
If we're not, then we should, but if we are, then we should.

211
01:15:25,160 --> 01:15:30,160
I know this is very common reasoning for people, and it seems to be, like,

212
01:15:30,160 --> 01:15:36,160
what else can you say other than status quo bias of thinking whatever is is a good idea,

213
01:15:36,160 --> 01:15:37,160
and we should keep doing it.

214
01:15:37,160 --> 01:15:42,160
So the very fact that you know that it is being done changes your mind about whether it's worthwhile

215
01:15:42,160 --> 01:15:48,160
and in the direction of what is is worthwhile.

216
01:15:48,160 --> 01:15:53,160
In particular, of course, in experiments, you can go and get transactions costs down to a very low level

217
01:15:53,160 --> 01:15:55,160
to really confirm it.

218
01:15:55,160 --> 01:16:00,160
In the real world, there is always the issue of maybe it's just transactions costs that explain it.

219
01:16:00,160 --> 01:16:01,160
So that is a problem.

220
01:16:01,160 --> 01:16:07,160
Of course, you can try in the real world to look at cases where the transaction costs seem unusually low,

221
01:16:07,160 --> 01:16:10,160
but still there's always a doubt in the real world, maybe it's just caused by transactions costs,

222
01:16:10,160 --> 01:16:15,160
which is another explanation for why people don't want to change things around.

223
01:16:15,160 --> 01:16:19,160
Questions?

224
01:16:19,160 --> 01:16:22,160
When you talk about how things become more popular by being designated as the status quo,

225
01:16:22,160 --> 01:16:29,160
so would you file the anchoring effect under this, like the first number of people here tends to, like,

226
01:16:29,160 --> 01:16:30,160
they're all there.

227
01:16:30,160 --> 01:16:32,160
At least it's very similar, yeah.

228
01:16:32,160 --> 01:16:36,160
So there's a set of experiments that I'm going to discuss where you just spin a wheel right in front of a person

229
01:16:36,160 --> 01:16:41,160
or they throw some dice, you know it's random, and then you say, all right, here's the number.

230
01:16:41,160 --> 01:16:46,160
Do you think that the next person who walks in that room was going to weigh more or less than that number?

231
01:16:46,160 --> 01:16:53,160
And it seems like even though you know it's random, the random number changes your view about what's going to happen.

232
01:16:53,160 --> 01:16:56,160
This is usually called anchoring because it's like, well, I mean, yeah, it's random,

233
01:16:56,160 --> 01:17:01,160
so it's not going to be exactly what the dice said, but it's not going to be more than, like, 20% more than what the dice said,

234
01:17:01,160 --> 01:17:03,160
is it?

235
01:17:03,160 --> 01:17:06,160
It's like, well, that makes no sense at all.

236
01:17:06,160 --> 01:17:12,160
The dice are random, so how could, like, why would your view about what's going to happen have anything to do with this?

237
01:17:12,160 --> 01:17:15,160
And yet people seem like they do.

238
01:17:15,160 --> 01:17:22,160
As I said, in the real world it's always easy to say that the endowment effect and status quo bias are illusory.

239
01:17:22,160 --> 01:17:26,160
And you say, look, it's either transactions cost or maybe it's just an information cost.

240
01:17:26,160 --> 01:17:31,160
So, you know, the status quo option usually gets to be the status quo option because most people like it.

241
01:17:31,160 --> 01:17:34,160
So think about the toppings on a Big Mac.

242
01:17:34,160 --> 01:17:36,160
Why are those the toppings on a Big Mac?

243
01:17:36,160 --> 01:17:38,160
Probably.

244
01:17:38,160 --> 01:17:39,160
What is it?

245
01:17:39,160 --> 01:17:43,160
Special sauce, au froid, you know, like anyone memorized.

246
01:17:43,160 --> 01:17:46,160
There used to be a commercial where they listed all the toppings on a Big Mac.

247
01:17:46,160 --> 01:17:47,160
All right.

248
01:17:47,160 --> 01:17:50,160
Well, how do McDonald's decide those would be the default toppings?

249
01:17:50,160 --> 01:17:52,160
They tested it out.

250
01:17:52,160 --> 01:17:55,160
Probably they tested it out and find out what is it that most people like.

251
01:17:55,160 --> 01:18:02,160
And then when you go and receive that offer, one reason to say, well, I'll do whatever you say,

252
01:18:02,160 --> 01:18:08,160
is if you think that they have deliberately picked out a set of options that most people like, and you say, well, I like most people,

253
01:18:08,160 --> 01:18:12,160
so I'll probably like what most people like.

254
01:18:12,160 --> 01:18:19,160
And there's actually a separate literature on trying to figure out or trying to forecast your own satisfaction.

255
01:18:19,160 --> 01:18:26,160
And one common rule of thumb that's been offered is instead of thinking about what you like, to ask the waiter what most people like.

256
01:18:26,160 --> 01:18:31,160
And at least for some purposes, it is more predictive to do what most people like, according to an expert,

257
01:18:31,160 --> 01:18:35,160
than to do what you think you will personally like.

258
01:18:35,160 --> 01:18:39,160
To me, this result has never been at all plausible because I'm so weird.

259
01:18:39,160 --> 01:18:43,160
But I can believe it works for other people.

260
01:18:43,160 --> 01:18:44,160
Okay.

261
01:18:44,160 --> 01:18:45,160
All right.

262
01:18:45,160 --> 01:18:49,160
So normally there's some information conveyed by something being the status quo.

263
01:18:49,160 --> 01:18:51,160
Like suppose that you believe in America.

264
01:18:51,160 --> 01:18:52,160
So you just believe in America.

265
01:18:52,160 --> 01:18:54,160
America does good stuff.

266
01:18:54,160 --> 01:18:59,160
In that case, if you find out that America has troops in Syria, what's a natural inference?

267
01:18:59,160 --> 01:19:00,160
It's a good idea.

268
01:19:00,160 --> 01:19:02,160
America, why did they decide to have troops in Syria?

269
01:19:02,160 --> 01:19:04,160
And you find out America does not have troops in Syria.

270
01:19:04,160 --> 01:19:06,160
What is a natural inference?

271
01:19:06,160 --> 01:19:09,160
America, why did they decide to not have troops in Syria?

272
01:19:09,160 --> 01:19:15,160
So in the real world, if you think there is a process that tends to select right answers,

273
01:19:15,160 --> 01:19:20,160
then whatever the status quo is, it's natural to go and believe in that.

274
01:19:20,160 --> 01:19:29,160
So that is a general problem if you think that there is something very functional about the status quo.

275
01:19:29,160 --> 01:19:31,160
All right.

276
01:19:31,160 --> 01:19:35,160
And then as I said, most experiments do focus on these close choices.

277
01:19:35,160 --> 01:19:39,160
So the question, if you need someone 100 coffee cups, how would they respond?

278
01:19:39,160 --> 01:19:41,160
Wouldn't they sell most of the coffee cups?

279
01:19:41,160 --> 01:19:46,160
And I don't think that experiment was ever done, but let's see.

280
01:19:46,160 --> 01:19:49,160
I'm thinking about his name.

281
01:19:49,160 --> 01:19:51,160
Let's see.

282
01:19:51,160 --> 01:19:54,160
Field experiments guy, Chicago.

283
01:19:54,160 --> 01:19:55,160
John List.

284
01:19:55,160 --> 01:19:56,160
Yeah, John List.

285
01:19:56,160 --> 01:19:57,160
Yes.

286
01:19:57,160 --> 01:20:00,160
I wanted to say Jonathan Fast, who is a former student here, and is not in person.


