01:25:44,440 --> 01:25:47,000
But even there, you say, well, who's more unselfish?

131
01:25:47,000 --> 01:25:49,240
The person who helps the orphans out of love or the person

132
01:25:49,240 --> 01:25:51,080
helps them because they feel like

133
01:25:51,080 --> 01:25:54,040
Manuel Kant proved they have to do it.

134
01:25:54,040 --> 01:25:56,240
At least it's an interesting,

135
01:25:56,240 --> 01:25:57,680
you're like, well, I don't know which one is

136
01:25:57,680 --> 01:26:01,080
the more unselfish according to normal English.

137
01:26:01,200 --> 01:26:05,720
Okay. So when economists use economics to explain the world,

138
01:26:05,720 --> 01:26:09,000
we normally do assume human selfishness in a narrow sense.

139
01:26:09,000 --> 01:26:12,120
You ever heard of Don McGraw's story,

140
01:26:12,120 --> 01:26:13,840
a question about kidneys?

141
01:26:13,840 --> 01:26:16,800
Comes down to this. Can I have your kidney for free?

142
01:26:16,800 --> 01:26:17,520
No.

143
01:26:17,520 --> 01:26:20,640
What if I need it? But I need your kidney for free. Can I just have it?

144
01:26:20,640 --> 01:26:21,640
I need my kidney.

145
01:26:21,640 --> 01:26:22,360
Yes.

146
01:26:22,360 --> 01:26:23,160
What?

147
01:26:23,160 --> 01:26:24,200
I need my kidney.

148
01:26:24,200 --> 01:26:25,600
Yeah. Well, actually,

149
01:26:25,600 --> 01:26:27,520
a doctor would tell you that you only need one.

150
01:26:27,520 --> 01:26:30,960
You've got two. Just like elective surgery.

151
01:26:30,960 --> 01:26:31,720
It's no big deal.

152
01:26:31,720 --> 01:26:34,480
Like one in 10,000 death rates,

153
01:26:34,480 --> 01:26:36,080
it's like an auto accident per year,

154
01:26:36,080 --> 01:26:37,720
and like I'd like a kidney.

155
01:26:37,720 --> 01:26:43,400
So very few people are inclined to give a stranger their kidney for free.

156
01:26:43,400 --> 01:26:45,800
And they would say, well,

157
01:26:45,800 --> 01:26:48,920
like this would really turn the recipient's life around.

158
01:26:48,920 --> 01:26:51,440
I think I'll still won't do it.

159
01:26:51,440 --> 01:26:53,960
It isn't a normal human reaction.

160
01:26:53,960 --> 01:26:55,960
There are a few people like Dylan Matthews and

161
01:26:55,960 --> 01:26:59,320
Vox when he gave his kidney away to a total stranger.

162
01:26:59,320 --> 01:27:03,280
So this is a case where someone says he's a utilitarian,

163
01:27:03,280 --> 01:27:06,800
and he appears to act like that is very consistent with doing that.

164
01:27:06,800 --> 01:27:09,120
But most people aren't like that.

165
01:27:09,120 --> 01:27:11,320
Okay. All right.

166
01:27:11,320 --> 01:27:14,320
Now, a slight variation that economists have sometimes brought in,

167
01:27:14,320 --> 01:27:16,400
especially when they're doing economics with family,

168
01:27:16,400 --> 01:27:23,280
is to go over biology and talk about inclusive fitness.

169
01:27:23,280 --> 01:27:26,760
All right. So how many of you here have ever read The Selfish Gene?

170
01:27:26,760 --> 01:27:28,880
Where's your dog in this? A few people.

171
01:27:28,880 --> 01:27:32,000
All right. So this is

172
01:27:32,000 --> 01:27:35,480
a simple Darwinian theory about who you really love,

173
01:27:35,480 --> 01:27:37,960
and who you are just using.

174
01:27:37,960 --> 01:27:40,640
And the story is, well,

175
01:27:40,640 --> 01:27:43,760
suppose that cavemen were willing to go and

176
01:27:43,760 --> 01:27:46,320
give their food away for free to total strangers.

177
01:27:46,320 --> 01:27:51,160
All right. So what's this going to do to their number of surviving descendants?

178
01:27:51,160 --> 01:27:53,880
It's going to hurt it. Right?

179
01:27:53,880 --> 01:27:57,080
On the other hand, suppose you had a caveman who had his own flesh and blood,

180
01:27:57,080 --> 01:27:59,280
his own biological children, and said,

181
01:27:59,280 --> 01:28:02,040
meh, you're never going to do anything for me.

182
01:28:02,040 --> 01:28:03,360
Right? Dead and no way going.

183
01:28:03,360 --> 01:28:06,960
What would happen to the children of those cavemen?

184
01:28:06,960 --> 01:28:09,880
Also, not many of them survive.

185
01:28:09,880 --> 01:28:13,360
So what is selected by evolution?

186
01:28:13,360 --> 01:28:16,400
It is to not care much about strangers,

187
01:28:16,400 --> 01:28:19,520
but to care a lot about your own biological relatives.

188
01:28:19,520 --> 01:28:22,960
Right? And in particular, inclusive fitness says that

189
01:28:22,960 --> 01:28:28,080
the genetic equilibrium is one where the genes are maximized.

190
01:28:28,080 --> 01:28:30,520
And this occurs when you go and value

191
01:28:30,520 --> 01:28:33,680
other people based upon how genetically related they are to you.

192
01:28:33,680 --> 01:28:36,160
So basically this means if you can,

193
01:28:36,160 --> 01:28:37,320
so each of your children,

194
01:28:37,320 --> 01:28:39,280
you share half their genes.

195
01:28:39,280 --> 01:28:43,160
So it says you'd be different between your own life and two kids.

196
01:28:43,160 --> 01:28:46,400
Or your own life and eight cousins.

197
01:28:46,400 --> 01:28:49,840
Right? Or your own life and four half-siblings.

198
01:28:49,840 --> 01:28:51,960
Sound right? All right.

199
01:28:51,960 --> 01:28:56,000
That is what inclusive fitness is all about.

200
01:28:56,000 --> 01:28:57,840
It does explain a lot.

201
01:28:57,840 --> 01:28:58,960
If you think about it more,

202
01:28:58,960 --> 01:29:01,040
you will notice some anomalies to that too.

203
01:29:01,040 --> 01:29:03,480
But anyway, there are some economists who have added

204
01:29:03,480 --> 01:29:05,240
an inclusive fitness and say, well,

205
01:29:05,240 --> 01:29:09,040
when someone goes and is generally selfish,

206
01:29:09,040 --> 01:29:12,240
but they lavish stuff on their own biological kids,

207
01:29:12,240 --> 01:29:15,400
we're still going to call them selfish, broadly speaking.

208
01:29:15,400 --> 01:29:17,960
And again, I would say this is not so weird.

209
01:29:17,960 --> 01:29:21,520
So imagine, you know, think about all the TV shows where there's an axe murderer.

210
01:29:21,520 --> 01:29:23,560
And as usual, the axe murderer's dad is

211
01:29:23,560 --> 01:29:27,880
a billionaire who buys him the best legal defense he could possibly get.

212
01:29:27,880 --> 01:29:29,800
And you go and confront the family,

213
01:29:29,800 --> 01:29:31,960
the victim is going to confront the billionaire and say,

214
01:29:31,960 --> 01:29:35,560
you're a horrible person trying to put your axe murdering son back on the street.

215
01:29:35,560 --> 01:29:37,920
And then the billionaire says,

216
01:29:37,920 --> 01:29:40,240
I love my son.

217
01:29:40,240 --> 01:29:42,920
All right. The point of this is,

218
01:29:42,920 --> 01:29:46,320
are we supposed to think of the billionaire trying to put his axe murdering son back on

219
01:29:46,320 --> 01:29:51,280
the street as being a selfish jerk or being a wonderful loving parent?

220
01:29:51,520 --> 01:29:54,760
Normally think of them as a selfish jerk because sure,

221
01:29:54,760 --> 01:29:56,160
they do love their own kid,

222
01:29:56,160 --> 01:30:00,760
but they don't care at all about all the people that their kid is going to murder with his axe.

223
01:30:00,760 --> 01:30:05,560
So that is a sense in which you might say that inclusive fitness,

224
01:30:05,560 --> 01:30:08,400
it's still basically in the same general realm of human selfishness.

225
01:30:08,400 --> 01:30:12,200
Or you know, you found out that some guy in the mafia gave his daughter a really nice car,

226
01:30:12,200 --> 01:30:15,200
would you say, oh my God, I've totally rethinked my view of this man?

227
01:30:15,200 --> 01:30:19,680
No, it's like, well yeah, well that's just what you'd expect a mafia boss to be like.

228
01:30:19,680 --> 01:30:23,120
Murders his enemies, but buys his daughter a great car.

229
01:30:23,120 --> 01:30:27,280
It doesn't change my view about him as a villain at all.

230
01:30:27,640 --> 01:30:33,680
Okay. Now, as you might remember from week six,

231
01:30:33,680 --> 01:30:37,080
when there is repeated play in a game,

232
01:30:37,080 --> 01:30:41,440
then totally selfish non-relatives will still cooperate.

233
01:30:41,440 --> 01:30:43,760
Why? Because of reciprocity.

234
01:30:43,760 --> 01:30:46,760
Right? You're cooperating in order to get something.

235
01:30:46,760 --> 01:30:52,280
So there's that. So mere cooperation is not good evidence against human selfishness.

236
01:30:52,280 --> 01:30:55,480
You need to focus on cooperation where one person gets

237
01:30:55,480 --> 01:31:00,440
an endless stream of free stuff and the other one knows they're not going to get anything back.

238
01:31:00,440 --> 01:31:05,720
That is the clear case where it's not just implicit trade.

239
01:31:05,720 --> 01:31:08,240
Right? Whereas most relationships,

240
01:31:08,240 --> 01:31:10,640
even once you think about between friends,

241
01:31:10,640 --> 01:31:14,080
they fit the model of implicit trade pretty well.

242
01:31:14,080 --> 01:31:19,400
Like think about cases where there's two friends and one of them always helps the other one,

243
01:31:19,400 --> 01:31:21,200
and the other one never reciprocates.

244
01:31:21,200 --> 01:31:23,440
How stable are friendships like that,

245
01:31:23,440 --> 01:31:26,040
where it's totally one way or like that?

246
01:31:26,040 --> 01:31:29,520
So generally going forward, those seem to be very stable friendships.

247
01:31:29,520 --> 01:31:32,320
Stable friendships are ones where there's reciprocity,

248
01:31:32,320 --> 01:31:34,160
where each side is able to help the other.

249
01:31:34,160 --> 01:31:37,280
And of course, it doesn't need to be to balance out every single day.

250
01:31:37,280 --> 01:31:42,360
But if 10 years have gone by and the one person who gives has never gotten anything back,

251
01:31:42,360 --> 01:31:46,640
that's a case where you'll start to wonder what is going on there.

252
01:31:46,640 --> 01:31:53,200
Right? All right. So questions about that? Yes.

253
01:31:53,200 --> 01:31:55,560
But if the toxic friendship goes on for 10 years,

254
01:31:55,560 --> 01:31:58,280
it's probably going to last because it's your toxic friend.

255
01:31:58,280 --> 01:32:01,480
So you could just save it with that tautology or you could say

256
01:32:01,480 --> 01:32:05,480
maybe this is a case where the selfish relationship story doesn't really work.

257
01:32:05,480 --> 01:32:08,400
Again, like the really clear case would be adoption.

258
01:32:08,400 --> 01:32:10,880
Right? So most people adopt a baby at birth,

259
01:32:10,880 --> 01:32:13,720
love the baby exactly as if it were their own biological child.

260
01:32:13,720 --> 01:32:16,920
And what does Inclusive Fitness say they should do with this baby?

261
01:32:16,920 --> 01:32:19,640
Say they will do, not should, but will do.

262
01:32:19,640 --> 01:32:22,400
Love it one over six million.

263
01:32:22,400 --> 01:32:24,560
Yes. So you'll send it back like,

264
01:32:24,560 --> 01:32:26,960
what are you going to do for me? We don't share any genes.

265
01:32:26,960 --> 01:32:28,960
Right? So now again,

266
01:32:28,960 --> 01:32:32,200
if you think about evolution more broadly,

267
01:32:32,200 --> 01:32:35,920
it's realizing well, a lot of our evolutionary impulses come from

268
01:32:35,920 --> 01:32:39,680
environments where you would never meet an unrelated baby.

269
01:32:39,680 --> 01:32:41,800
Right? So like in a primitive tribe,

270
01:32:41,800 --> 01:32:44,840
if someone dies and they have a baby that's around,

271
01:32:44,840 --> 01:32:47,640
what are the odds that you are actually a close relative of that baby?

272
01:32:47,640 --> 01:32:48,200
Very high.

273
01:32:48,200 --> 01:32:49,320
Yeah, really high, right?

274
01:32:49,320 --> 01:32:51,600
Because there's only 30 people in this band.

275
01:32:51,600 --> 01:32:54,960
You're probably related to that baby 17 different ways.

276
01:32:54,960 --> 01:32:58,560
Right? Yes, you think of it,

277
01:32:58,560 --> 01:33:03,000
the level of incest in a primitive tribe is really high when you think about it.

278
01:33:03,000 --> 01:33:04,000
Woof, woof.

279
01:33:04,000 --> 01:33:06,920
Yes. All right.

280
01:33:06,920 --> 01:33:11,760
Okay. So anyway, so mere cooperation is not good evidence against human selfishness,

281
01:33:11,760 --> 01:33:15,120
and again, remember Thomas Hobbes and we talked about him?

282
01:33:15,120 --> 01:33:17,000
The idea that everyone were totally selfish,

283
01:33:17,000 --> 01:33:19,760
there'd be no cooperation is a little bit ridiculous.

284
01:33:19,760 --> 01:33:22,960
Like there's, there's cooperating out there is reciprocity,

285
01:33:22,960 --> 01:33:26,080
and that's enough to go and sustain cooperation in a lot of situations.

286
01:33:26,080 --> 01:33:28,120
But still, in the real world,

287
01:33:28,120 --> 01:33:32,160
it seems like we're seeing more reciprocity than selfishness predicts,

288
01:33:32,160 --> 01:33:36,960
and there's a bunch of experiments that have shown that you can replicate this in lab.

289
01:33:36,960 --> 01:33:41,000
Right? Right? People often cooperate,

290
01:33:41,000 --> 01:33:42,920
even in one-shot prisoner of solemnness.

291
01:33:42,920 --> 01:33:48,320
There's no repeat. And you can even, excuse me,

292
01:33:48,320 --> 01:33:50,160
you can give full anonymity.

293
01:33:50,160 --> 01:33:52,320
So everybody's got, everybody knows,

294
01:33:52,320 --> 01:33:54,400
like you'll do them, no idea who the other person is,

295
01:33:54,400 --> 01:33:56,440
or your partner is, you don't know who's stabbing you in the back,

296
01:33:56,440 --> 01:33:59,560
and yet there's still these modest levels of cooperation.

297
01:33:59,560 --> 01:34:02,680
And why? Why? Why would you guys cooperate?

298
01:34:02,680 --> 01:34:05,640
I mean, like, common answer would be because it's the right thing to do.

299
01:34:05,640 --> 01:34:06,960
I'm a nice person.

300
01:34:06,960 --> 01:34:09,040
And I, you know, and you might say,

301
01:34:09,040 --> 01:34:11,080
well, I'm doing this because I trust other people.

302
01:34:11,080 --> 01:34:13,920
And it's like, well, but if you, you know,

303
01:34:13,920 --> 01:34:16,680
if an evil person trusted other people,

304
01:34:16,680 --> 01:34:18,480
he still wouldn't cooperate, would he?

305
01:34:18,480 --> 01:34:21,920
If it's one shot and no one ever knows who you are,

306
01:34:21,920 --> 01:34:23,800
then you go, yeah, I trust other people.

307
01:34:23,800 --> 01:34:25,440
We're not a bunch of idiots.

308
01:34:25,440 --> 01:34:27,560
I'm going to go and rip them all off.

309
01:34:27,560 --> 01:34:31,680
Ha ha. All right.

310
01:34:31,680 --> 01:34:34,320
And so what is the obvious explanation?

311
01:34:34,320 --> 01:34:38,960
Why would someone cooperate when you've removed every possible selfish reason to do so?

312
01:34:38,960 --> 01:34:41,520
For an unselfish reason.

313
01:34:41,520 --> 01:34:45,480
Because they care. Because they care about other human beings to some extent.

314
01:34:45,480 --> 01:34:49,520
And in Superman 2, there's the villain General Zod.

315
01:34:49,520 --> 01:34:53,640
Not the pale imitation of General Zod in the more recent Superman movie,

316
01:34:53,640 --> 01:34:57,560
unwatchable, but the original General Zod played by Heron Stamp,

317
01:34:57,560 --> 01:35:01,680
who, you know, a towering figure in super villainy.

318
01:35:01,680 --> 01:35:04,880
And there's one part where, you know,

319
01:35:04,880 --> 01:35:07,800
like the three villains from Krypton are fighting Superman.

320
01:35:07,800 --> 01:35:12,520
And then Superman desperately rushes to go and prevent some humans from getting killed.

321
01:35:12,520 --> 01:35:15,840
And then I think General Zod says,

322
01:35:15,840 --> 01:35:18,000
I've discovered Superman's weakness.

323
01:35:18,000 --> 01:35:19,520
And what is it?

324
01:35:19,520 --> 01:35:22,640
He cares for these things.

325
01:35:22,640 --> 01:35:25,520
And the other Kryptonian says,

326
01:35:25,520 --> 01:35:28,480
his pets. And he is presuming.

327
01:35:28,480 --> 01:35:31,040
So to the villains from Krypton,

328
01:35:31,040 --> 01:35:36,440
the idea that Superman might care about a human being that serves no useful function for him,

329
01:35:36,440 --> 01:35:39,200
no selfish function is just weird, but

330
01:35:39,200 --> 01:35:41,800
they observe and say, hmm, how odd.

331
01:35:41,800 --> 01:35:44,720
I conducted a controlled experiment by throwing a bus

332
01:35:44,720 --> 01:35:47,280
at those people and he made an effort to save them.

333
01:35:47,280 --> 01:35:50,280
What could they possibly do for him? Nothing.

334
01:35:50,280 --> 01:35:53,240
There's only one logical inference left to draw.

335
01:35:53,240 --> 01:35:55,240
He likes them.

336
01:35:57,240 --> 01:36:01,400
All right. So a lot of this evidence on cooperation in

337
01:36:01,400 --> 01:36:04,400
Prisoner of Solemnus comes from public goods experiments.

338
01:36:04,400 --> 01:36:06,480
So even in one-shot games,

339
01:36:06,480 --> 01:36:10,240
it's very common for there to be 46 percent cooperation.

340
01:36:10,240 --> 01:36:12,440
When you do repeated play,

341
01:36:12,440 --> 01:36:15,200
what you usually see is there's actually a decline in cooperation.

342
01:36:15,200 --> 01:36:17,040
Because some people realize,

343
01:36:17,040 --> 01:36:18,920
oh my God, my partners are not being

344
01:36:18,920 --> 01:36:22,320
fair and they're cheating me so I'm going to stop cooperating too.

345
01:36:22,320 --> 01:36:25,280
Right? But there's also a common result that you can get

346
01:36:25,280 --> 01:36:27,320
cooperation up to a higher level again just by saying,

347
01:36:27,320 --> 01:36:29,520
all right, new tournament.

348
01:36:29,520 --> 01:36:31,640
And just like that,

349
01:36:31,640 --> 01:36:33,560
again, that takes up just relabeling things.

350
01:36:33,560 --> 01:36:36,520
New round. And they're like, oh my God, it's a new round.

351
01:36:36,520 --> 01:36:38,280
Right? What has changed?

352
01:36:38,280 --> 01:36:40,040
You're still playing with the same people.

353
01:36:40,040 --> 01:36:42,240
Still, almost all the incentives are the same just because

354
01:36:42,240 --> 01:36:44,840
the experimenter declares new round, this changes things.

355
01:36:44,840 --> 01:36:48,160
But it seems like it gives people hope that people will turn over

356
01:36:48,160 --> 01:36:52,880
a new leaf and they will start cooperating because, let's see.

357
01:36:52,880 --> 01:36:56,240
So I think when Hillary Clinton became Secretary of State,

358
01:36:56,240 --> 01:36:57,480
she said she was going to press

359
01:36:57,480 --> 01:36:59,440
the reset button on relations with Russia.

360
01:36:59,440 --> 01:37:02,200
I think they actually got a button and gave to

361
01:37:02,200 --> 01:37:04,080
her as a gift and pressed the button and said,

362
01:37:04,080 --> 01:37:06,160
let's press the reset button together.

363
01:37:06,160 --> 01:37:09,000
And it didn't seem to work in this case.

364
01:37:09,000 --> 01:37:12,040
So the experiment lacked external validity.

365
01:37:12,040 --> 01:37:13,840
But, there you are.

366
01:37:13,840 --> 01:37:17,440
All right. Now, there's a lot of extra experimental elements too.

367
01:37:17,440 --> 01:37:19,360
So how about tipping?

368
01:37:19,360 --> 01:37:22,000
So, why tip?

369
01:37:22,000 --> 01:37:24,280
Now, if you go back to the same restaurant frequently,

370
01:37:24,280 --> 01:37:25,880
you might say it's to get better service because

371
01:37:25,880 --> 01:37:28,280
you think the servers will remember whether you're a good or bad tipper.

372
01:37:28,280 --> 01:37:31,600
What about whenever you're never going to go back to that restaurant again?

373
01:37:31,600 --> 01:37:33,320
Do you still tip?

374
01:37:33,320 --> 01:37:34,000
Yes.

375
01:37:34,000 --> 01:37:35,120
Oh yeah, sure you do.

376
01:37:35,120 --> 01:37:37,600
Now, we know for the data, almost everybody tips.

377
01:37:37,600 --> 01:37:39,880
So I have asked people who work at these restaurants,

378
01:37:39,880 --> 01:37:42,320
how often does someone simply give no tip at all?

379
01:37:42,320 --> 01:37:44,720
And I think it's one percent of the time.

380
01:37:44,720 --> 01:37:47,000
This is even people who do work with

