01:58:22,280 --> 01:58:23,360
Is it just merit?

509
01:58:23,360 --> 01:58:25,920
No, it's gaming the system and making more of you.

510
01:58:27,240 --> 01:58:29,560
But, so that's a reason why you might try to get

511
01:58:29,560 --> 01:58:33,280
your very, like your personal students good jobs,

512
01:58:33,280 --> 01:58:35,320
but again, 95% of lawyers' recommendation

513
01:58:35,320 --> 01:58:37,640
are nothing like this, and selfishly,

514
01:58:37,640 --> 01:58:39,080
it's a total waste of time for the professor,

515
01:58:39,080 --> 01:58:41,000
but most of us do it anyway.

516
01:58:41,920 --> 01:58:44,560
Most of us do it anyway, out of some sense

517
01:58:44,560 --> 01:58:47,680
of I'm the professor, and fairness requires

518
01:58:47,680 --> 01:58:49,160
that I do this, and what kind of a jerk

519
01:58:49,160 --> 01:58:50,960
would I be if I didn't do it?

520
01:58:52,440 --> 01:58:54,280
The fairness norms are much weaker for

521
01:58:54,280 --> 01:58:56,400
do I have to ever referee an article?

522
01:58:56,400 --> 01:58:59,160
And the norm has been going down, down, down

523
01:58:59,160 --> 01:59:00,960
for what's actually what people feel like they have to do,

524
01:59:00,960 --> 01:59:02,920
and I think we are sort of at the point where

525
01:59:02,920 --> 01:59:04,080
most people just feel like they don't have

526
01:59:04,080 --> 01:59:05,340
to referee at all.

527
01:59:05,340 --> 01:59:07,280
But then again, I will still hear about famous people

528
01:59:07,280 --> 01:59:08,760
who spent, who took a week out of their life

529
01:59:08,760 --> 01:59:10,080
to referee an article, and I'm like,

530
01:59:10,080 --> 01:59:12,660
I can't believe this person has been

531
01:59:12,660 --> 01:59:14,680
half as successful as they are

532
01:59:14,680 --> 01:59:18,280
spending 60 hours on something with no real payoff for them.

533
01:59:19,160 --> 01:59:21,400
And possibly if you're at that elite level,

534
01:59:21,400 --> 01:59:22,840
then again, there's some kind of implicit

535
01:59:22,840 --> 01:59:25,080
payroof rating, and editors will reward you

536
01:59:25,080 --> 01:59:27,520
for your detailed report by giving you

537
01:59:27,520 --> 01:59:30,080
good referees, subsequently, maybe.

538
01:59:31,360 --> 01:59:32,720
All right.

539
01:59:32,720 --> 01:59:34,360
Okay, so I mean, if that's fairness for you,

540
01:59:34,360 --> 01:59:35,360
questions about it?

541
01:59:36,440 --> 01:59:37,960
Please, all right.

542
01:59:37,960 --> 01:59:39,600
So in particular, when I hear about,

543
01:59:39,600 --> 01:59:41,240
not in economics, of course, where we're all,

544
01:59:41,240 --> 01:59:43,740
all the teachers are great, but in other departments,

545
01:59:43,740 --> 01:59:46,520
I've heard about some incredible levels

546
01:59:46,520 --> 01:59:50,160
of laziness and neglect, and how many students

547
01:59:50,160 --> 01:59:53,320
actually even will give the professor

548
01:59:53,320 --> 01:59:56,280
a four instead of a five on student evaluations

549
01:59:56,280 --> 01:60:16,280
to retaliate for the professor's laziness.


1
01:60:00,000 --> 01:60:02,000
Maybe 5, 10 percent.

2
01:60:02,000 --> 01:60:04,000
Everyone else still says, oh, this guy's great.

3
01:60:04,000 --> 01:60:08,000
Never passes back homework, rambles on, doesn't really expect anything.

4
01:60:08,000 --> 01:60:12,000
Yeah, but he's great. Yeah, excellent.

5
01:60:12,000 --> 01:60:14,000
All right.

6
01:60:14,000 --> 01:60:25,000
Now, a second motive that we see, which is also not in a standard model of selfish behavior is vindictiveness.

7
01:60:25,000 --> 01:60:30,000
The desire for revenge.

8
01:60:30,000 --> 01:60:34,000
And we can see this very clearly in the ultimatum game.

9
01:60:34,000 --> 01:60:45,000
If you go and give significantly less than half in the ultimatum game, a lot of people will reject your offer, reducing their payoff and yours.

10
01:60:45,000 --> 01:60:48,000
And what possible motive could they have?

11
01:60:48,000 --> 01:60:54,000
Well, again, in an experiment, you can go and narrow it down so they don't know who you are, they don't know anything about you, they'll never find out.

12
01:60:54,000 --> 01:61:03,000
So why go and give up $4 to go and make the other player $6 poorer?

13
01:61:03,000 --> 01:61:06,000
Because you hate that person.

14
01:61:06,000 --> 01:61:08,000
Because you are angry.

15
01:61:08,000 --> 01:61:11,000
I want to send them a message. What's the message?

16
01:61:11,000 --> 01:61:13,000
I don't like you.

17
01:61:13,000 --> 01:61:16,000
You made me mad. You hurt my feelings.

18
01:61:16,000 --> 01:61:19,000
And now I'm going to hurt your feelings.

19
01:61:19,000 --> 01:61:24,000
You thought you could rip me off, but you can't.

20
01:61:24,000 --> 01:61:27,000
So there's that.

21
01:61:27,000 --> 01:61:30,000
And this is true especially when we've been treated unfairly.

22
01:61:30,000 --> 01:61:32,000
So there's a connection between fairness and vindictiveness.

23
01:61:32,000 --> 01:61:38,000
One of the most common reasons people have to act vindictively is that they think that the other side acted unfairly.

24
01:61:38,000 --> 01:61:45,000
And so now I am willing to lose something in order to make you suffer.

25
01:61:45,000 --> 01:61:52,000
Although, it's unclear whether you want to call it vindictiveness, you might just call it spite.

26
01:61:52,000 --> 01:62:00,000
So there are some experiments where you give someone a chance to go and hurt another person who hasn't done anything to you.

27
01:62:00,000 --> 01:62:03,000
And a certain number of people will do that.

28
01:62:03,000 --> 01:62:10,000
And it's like, here's the most successful, basically you give a test, everybody goes and gets paid based on the performance of the test.

29
01:62:10,000 --> 01:62:15,000
Your performance is based purely on your performance.

30
01:62:15,000 --> 01:62:18,000
And then you go and ask people anonymously,

31
01:62:18,000 --> 01:62:22,000
would you give up a dollar to take a dollar away from the person who got the high score on the test?

32
01:62:22,000 --> 01:62:26,000
And a lot of people, maybe a third of people will say, yeah, hell yeah.

33
01:62:26,000 --> 01:62:29,000
I'm going to wipe that smug look right off his face.

34
01:62:29,000 --> 01:62:33,000
And you might say, why do you want to hurt this person?

35
01:62:33,000 --> 01:62:36,000
This person didn't get the money by doing anything to you.

36
01:62:36,000 --> 01:62:40,000
Like, if you had done better, you would have gotten just as much as he had.

37
01:62:40,000 --> 01:62:45,000
And it seems like, why should he have all the money and me not?

38
01:62:45,000 --> 01:62:48,000
Why, I ask you.

39
01:62:48,000 --> 01:62:52,000
I'm going to go and show him, does he think he's better than me?

40
01:62:52,000 --> 01:62:55,000
Well, he won't after I ruin his life.

41
01:62:55,000 --> 01:62:57,000
So there's that.

42
01:62:57,000 --> 01:63:00,000
That is another reason.

43
01:63:00,000 --> 01:63:02,000
It's just trying to hurt someone.

44
01:63:02,000 --> 01:63:08,000
So there's the Ayn Rand phrase, hatred of the good for being good.

45
01:63:08,000 --> 01:63:10,000
So I want to hurt those people. Why?

46
01:63:10,000 --> 01:63:14,000
Because they're successful and I'm not.

47
01:63:14,000 --> 01:63:20,000
And you might even, in perspective, find that you too have felt this on some occasion.

48
01:63:20,000 --> 01:63:26,000
You might say, well, it's because people believe the rule zero sum, but what if you make it totally clear?

49
01:63:26,000 --> 01:63:28,000
Like, it's not zero sum, it's positive sum.

50
01:63:28,000 --> 01:63:33,000
Now, I don't like the idea of anyone being more successful than me.

51
01:63:33,000 --> 01:63:43,000
Alright, now, notice that this is actually a psychological result where economists are being unduly optimistic about mankind.

52
01:63:43,000 --> 01:63:49,000
Because if everyone were purely selfish, how much energy would be put into spite?

53
01:63:49,000 --> 01:63:53,000
Anonymous spite. Non-repeat spite.

54
01:63:53,000 --> 01:63:56,000
You might act like you want revenge if it's going to change someone's behavior,

55
01:63:56,000 --> 01:64:01,000
but would you ever just lose money in order to hurt another person?

56
01:64:01,000 --> 01:64:03,000
No.

57
01:64:03,000 --> 01:64:05,000
But in the real world, people do this.

58
01:64:05,000 --> 01:64:15,000
So anyway, there's this long-standing idea that economists have this ridiculously negative view of human nature.

59
01:64:15,000 --> 01:64:21,000
But this is actually a way in which economists have an overly positive view of human nature.

60
01:64:21,000 --> 01:64:29,000
So, again, which is not really a point in our favor, but it just means that the critics are missing a lot of what's going on in the world.

61
01:64:29,000 --> 01:64:33,000
Before pointing that out.

62
01:64:33,000 --> 01:64:41,000
So, you just think about all the things that would never happen if economists were right about human nature.

63
01:64:41,000 --> 01:64:47,000
Like, how many murder-suicides would there be if economists were right about human nature?

64
01:64:47,000 --> 01:64:52,000
What would spur a person to a murder-suicide?

65
01:64:52,000 --> 01:64:55,000
Like, you made me really angry.

66
01:64:55,000 --> 01:65:02,000
So angry that I would rather be dead than live in a world where you're alive in it.

67
01:65:02,000 --> 01:65:06,000
And that does not sound at all like what an economist thinks.

68
01:65:06,000 --> 01:65:09,000
Of course, murder-suicides are rare, unfortunately.

69
01:65:09,000 --> 01:65:11,000
But they do happen.

70
01:65:11,000 --> 01:65:19,000
So, you could really stretch your imagination and say, well, this is some kind of effort to go and send a warning to people.

71
01:65:19,000 --> 01:65:22,000
But it's really quite a warning.

72
01:65:22,000 --> 01:65:25,000
So, with inclusive fitness, there could be a reason to do it, right?

73
01:65:25,000 --> 01:65:28,000
You might go and do this for the sake of your family.

74
01:65:28,000 --> 01:65:34,000
Like, someone goes and hurts your family, and maybe only if a month lives, you go and kill them and then kill yourself.

75
01:65:34,000 --> 01:65:37,000
And then, are people going to mess with your family after that?

76
01:65:37,000 --> 01:65:41,000
And, especially, suppose your family's reputation were doing this kind of thing.

77
01:65:41,000 --> 01:65:47,000
Like, we have a higher rate of murder-suicide in our family than any other family in the country.

78
01:65:47,000 --> 01:65:51,000
So, you ought to be really nice to us.

79
01:65:51,000 --> 01:65:54,000
Don't break our hearts. Don't fire us.

80
01:65:54,000 --> 01:65:59,000
Don't do anything that would make us question whether we want to be alive or not.

81
01:65:59,000 --> 01:66:04,000
So, you could go and tell that story, but it seems pretty far-fetched.

82
01:66:04,000 --> 01:66:11,000
I mean, obviously, in a world where the economic model of narrow human selfishness were true,

83
01:66:11,000 --> 01:66:14,000
there would be almost no suicide bombing or anything like that.

84
01:66:14,000 --> 01:66:18,000
So, there's a lot of the worst things that we actually see in the world

85
01:66:18,000 --> 01:66:24,000
that a model of pure human selfishness would say would not occur, actually.

86
01:66:24,000 --> 01:66:26,000
Here's another one.

87
01:66:26,000 --> 01:66:30,000
How much pure bullying would happen in a world where everyone was selfish?

88
01:66:30,000 --> 01:66:34,000
So, I mean, think about the bullies that you knew growing up. I assume none of you were the bully.

89
01:66:34,000 --> 01:66:37,000
Maybe, yeah, but even I bullied a couple of times.

90
01:66:37,000 --> 01:66:39,000
You're like, who can you bully?

91
01:66:39,000 --> 01:66:42,000
But, it did happen. But, anyway.

92
01:66:42,000 --> 01:66:46,000
So, how much money was actually made by the bullies?

93
01:66:46,000 --> 01:66:50,000
What financial gain? I mean, there's the stealing the lunch money bullying.

94
01:66:50,000 --> 01:66:55,000
So, there is that kind. But, most bullying is more of, I'm just going to be mean to a person.

95
01:66:55,000 --> 01:66:58,000
Right? And, what is in it for that person?

96
01:66:58,000 --> 01:67:01,000
The person who was doing the bullying, what did they get out of it?

97
01:67:01,000 --> 01:67:03,000
But, mostly it's just like sadistic pleasure.

98
01:67:03,000 --> 01:67:09,000
As well as the status among other people who also enjoy sadistic pleasures.

99
01:67:09,000 --> 01:67:13,000
And, like in a world where everyone were perfectly selfish,

100
01:67:13,000 --> 01:67:17,000
almost all this bullying would be wiped off the face of the earth.

101
01:67:17,000 --> 01:67:21,000
And, people would at least treat each other with indifference.

102
01:67:21,000 --> 01:67:24,000
It was the big improvement over what a lot of kids face, right?

103
01:67:24,000 --> 01:67:28,000
Just imagine if everyone who bullied you just acted like you were alive.

104
01:67:28,000 --> 01:67:31,000
It would be a big gain.

105
01:67:31,000 --> 01:67:36,000
Alright. So, alright. Now, real world significance of all this.

106
01:67:36,000 --> 01:67:40,000
Alright. So, easy to see this on some level.

107
01:67:40,000 --> 01:67:42,000
Alright. So, let's see.

108
01:67:42,000 --> 01:67:45,000
So, like, you know, even when there's no repeated interaction,

109
01:67:45,000 --> 01:67:48,000
we often give up small personal benefits to what fairness requires.

110
01:67:48,000 --> 01:67:51,000
Expect others to do the same.

111
01:67:51,000 --> 01:67:55,000
Alright. And, you know, some of the more convincing cases,

112
01:67:55,000 --> 01:67:59,000
so maybe prices and wages would move around more if these fairness norms are weaker.

113
01:67:59,000 --> 01:68:01,000
Especially wages. So, we'll get to that.

114
01:68:01,000 --> 01:68:05,000
It's very unusual for nominal wages to ever fall.

115
01:68:05,000 --> 01:68:07,000
Especially, like, their base nominal wage.

116
01:68:07,000 --> 01:68:10,000
Bonuses are more flexible, but base nominal wages,

117
01:68:10,000 --> 01:68:13,000
and it seems that it's heavily driven based upon a widespread perception

118
01:68:13,000 --> 01:68:16,000
that it's super unfair to ever cut someone's nominal wage by a penny or more,

119
01:68:16,000 --> 01:68:20,000
by a penny per hour, and that only a monster would do this.

120
01:68:20,000 --> 01:68:25,000
And, given that employers know that there's a very strong negative reaction to this,

121
01:68:25,000 --> 01:68:28,000
they're afraid of the predictiveness that they would provoke in the workers,

122
01:68:28,000 --> 01:68:33,000
and so we very rarely see noticeable changes in nominal pay.

123
01:68:33,000 --> 01:68:35,000
At least nominal wages.

124
01:68:35,000 --> 01:68:39,000
Alright. And then there are some other markets where they're notoriously,

125
01:68:39,000 --> 01:68:45,000
there are, like, prices are upwardly flexible, such as for concert tickets.

126
01:68:45,000 --> 01:68:51,000
So, there are at least some cases where the concert tickets are clearly underpriced,

127
01:68:51,000 --> 01:68:54,000
and then they get bought up really quickly, and then there's a rationing mechanism,

128
01:68:54,000 --> 01:68:57,000
but scalpers figure out a way around the rationing mechanism,

129
01:68:57,000 --> 01:69:00,000
and this is why you have to go and go through the security to show your monorobot

130
01:69:00,000 --> 01:69:03,000
that it's just buying up tickets to resell them.

131
01:69:03,000 --> 01:69:09,000
And again, why don't the original performers just go and raise the price?

132
01:69:09,000 --> 01:69:13,000
So, there are some complex profit-maximizing stories saying things like,

133
01:69:13,000 --> 01:69:17,000
well, they're concerned about not just their fans today, but the fans of the future,

134
01:69:17,000 --> 01:69:22,000
and if you just charge the highest possible prices, then you are alienating young fans,

135
01:69:22,000 --> 01:69:25,000
and the young fans are going to be around a lot longer,

136
01:69:25,000 --> 01:69:28,000
and so it's all a very complex profit-maximization problem that you plan,

137
01:69:28,000 --> 01:69:31,000
what is it you plan when you're 80 years old, and you're Simon and Garfunkel,

138
01:69:31,000 --> 01:69:35,000
you want the people who are grateful to you for the cheap tickets they got 60 years ago

139
01:69:35,000 --> 01:69:37,000
to come to your concert.

140
01:69:37,000 --> 01:69:40,000
Alright, so, there are some stories like that,

141
01:69:40,000 --> 01:69:46,000
but the simple one that people usually say is they just think it's unfair to charge that much.

142
01:69:46,000 --> 01:69:53,000
My brother once actually had Super Bowl tickets that he managed to get at the list price, like 200 bucks,

143
01:69:53,000 --> 01:69:58,000
and I think they got up to like $1,000, and then my brother did sell them.

144
01:69:58,000 --> 01:70:01,000
Although, even there, he was wavering.

145
01:70:01,000 --> 01:70:05,000
My brother's not an economist, so I would be the same, like, you know,

146
01:70:05,000 --> 01:70:13,000
I wouldn't pay $1,000, so why would I give up $1,000 when I could sell them for that?

147
01:70:13,000 --> 01:70:15,000
Makes sense to me.

148
01:70:19,000 --> 01:70:22,000
Alright, questions, questions, anyone happy?

149
01:70:22,000 --> 01:70:28,000
Alright, so, now, another oddity,

150
01:70:28,000 --> 01:70:31,000
this is one that's gotten quite a bit more attention in recent years,

151
01:70:31,000 --> 01:70:35,000
almost entirely through papers where James Heckman is a co-author.

152
01:70:35,000 --> 01:70:40,000
If you look at James Heckman's productivity, it is beyond all belief,

153
01:70:40,000 --> 01:70:44,000
and you'll notice that they're almost all co-authors, so I think that explains a great deal,

154
01:70:44,000 --> 01:70:49,000
but Heckman being famous and awesome in many ways can get co-authors that are really good,

155
01:70:49,000 --> 01:70:52,000
and they'll probably let him put his name on it for 5% of the work, say.

156
01:70:52,000 --> 01:71:00,000
Also, a lot of his co-authors are former students, so it may be that they are repaying his pedagogy.

157
01:71:00,000 --> 01:71:02,000
Possibly.

158
01:71:02,000 --> 01:71:08,000
Alright, so, there are a lot of economists who go beyond the assumption of constant preferences over time,

159
01:71:08,000 --> 01:71:11,000
constant preferences means over time,

160
01:71:11,000 --> 01:71:17,000
to the assumption of identical preferences for all humans.

161
01:71:17,000 --> 01:71:23,000
What? All humans have the same preferences?

162
01:71:23,000 --> 01:71:30,000
And yet, this is actually a view defended by two Nobel Prize winners, Stiglitz and Becker,

163
01:71:30,000 --> 01:71:33,000
in a paper that was extremely influential.

164
01:71:33,000 --> 01:71:39,000
And a lot of the argument came down to say, look, you can explain anything with preferences,

165
01:71:39,000 --> 01:71:42,000
like why did the man drink motor oil? Because he liked the taste of motor oil.

166
01:71:42,000 --> 01:71:44,000
Alright, that's pretty lame.

167
01:71:44,000 --> 01:71:52,000
So, instead of giving us, ourselves, the infinite room to explain differences between people by differences between preferences,

168
01:71:52,000 --> 01:72:01,000
why don't we go and look at different constraints that they face, or different income levels, and explain it that way.

169
01:72:01,000 --> 01:72:05,000
The kind of most plausible examples they would have is,

170
01:72:05,000 --> 01:72:12,000
suppose you see that people don't eat much ice cream during the winter, and they eat a lot of ice cream in the summer.

171
01:72:12,000 --> 01:72:18,000
Does this mean that the preference for ice cream changes over the year?

172
01:72:18,000 --> 01:72:22,000
And Becker and Stiglitz would want to say, no, there's a constant preference for ice cream,

173
01:72:22,000 --> 01:72:25,000
but your desire for ice cream depends upon the weather.

174
01:72:25,000 --> 01:72:30,000
So, the true fundamental preference is constant.

175
01:72:30,000 --> 01:72:37,000
But, the appearance may be flexible because your circumstances have changed.

176
01:72:37,000 --> 01:72:42,000
I mean, this would be really clear if it were, like, you want a snow shovel in the winter but not in the summer.

177
01:72:42,000 --> 01:72:46,000
Does this show that you like snow shovels in winter and don't like snow shovels in summer?

178
01:72:46,000 --> 01:72:51,000
No, it shows that you have no use for the snow shovel in the summer, but you have a use for the snow shovel in the winter.

179
01:72:51,000 --> 01:72:55,000
In both cases, your fundamental preference is for a drivable driveway.

180
01:72:55,000 --> 01:72:57,000
And that's what's constant.

181
01:72:57,000 --> 01:73:02,000
And then weather changes the constraints.

182
01:73:02,000 --> 01:73:14,000
And then they start, well, alright, what would you say about librarians seeming to be people who like jobs that are quiet,

183
01:73:14,000 --> 01:73:16,000
and like jobs where they talk?

184
01:73:16,000 --> 01:73:22,000
They want to say, no, no, no, what's going on here is that each kind of worker invested in a different kind of human capital.

185
01:73:22,000 --> 01:73:26,000
And now they invest in human capital, they are better.

186
01:73:26,000 --> 01:73:32,000
The librarian is better at producing librarian services, and the salesman is better at producing salesman services.

187
01:73:32,000 --> 01:73:35,000
But it's not because they have a fundamental difference in preference.

188
01:73:35,000 --> 01:73:39,000
These are two different ways of making money.

189
01:73:39,000 --> 01:73:48,000
And let's see, I think actually this is in a footnote, where there's this old story about why are there so many Jews in finance?

190
01:73:48,000 --> 01:73:54,000
And then there's, you say, well, Jews just like finance.

191
01:73:54,000 --> 01:73:56,000
So there's that one.

192
01:73:56,000 --> 01:74:02,000
And then, but then there's a story going back a much longer time saying, no, in the past there were a bunch of regulations

193
01:74:02,000 --> 01:74:07,000
which kept them out of all the other occupations, and finance was one of the few that was left.

194
01:74:07,000 --> 01:74:13,000
So they went there, and once they're there, it's easy for them to pass those job skills on to their kids and their kids' kids.

195
01:74:13,000 --> 01:74:15,000
And that's what's going on.

196
01:74:15,000 --> 01:74:20,000
And Stigler and Becker did say, yeah, that sounds pretty good, although on a footnote they said,

197
01:74:20,000 --> 01:74:23,000
that still doesn't explain why they just didn't convert to another religion.

198
01:74:23,000 --> 01:74:26,000
And they said, well, we'll just leave that for future research.

199
01:74:26,000 --> 01:74:34,000
But again, the spirit of it is to say, well, everyone would convert to whatever religion will actually give them the best deal.

200
01:74:34,000 --> 01:74:40,000
And so, and again, this is where many people just start saying, really?

201
01:74:40,000 --> 01:74:43,000
But anyway, can they really be saying what they appear to be saying?

202
01:74:43,000 --> 01:74:46,000
And I'll say, yes, they are saying what they appear to be saying.

203
01:74:46,000 --> 01:74:55,000
So whatever differences that appear to happen between people, it's really only on the surface that some people like one kind of music rather than another,

204
01:74:55,000 --> 01:74:57,000
and that's just a raw, unexplained thing.

205
01:74:57,000 --> 01:75:01,000
And instead, they say, no, no, what happened was that people like Chinese opera,

206
01:75:01,000 --> 01:75:07,000
early on they were in a situation where there was a lower cost of building up Chinese opera appreciation capital.

207
01:75:07,000 --> 01:75:13,000
And people who grew up liking rock and roll grew up in an environment where it was easy to acquire rock and roll appreciation capital,

208
01:75:13,000 --> 01:75:15,000
and their fundamental preference is identical.

209
01:75:15,000 --> 01:75:16,000
Yes?

210
01:75:16,000 --> 01:75:24,000
So the librarian, if I can just use this example, like they have skills now, but we call those preferences,

211
01:75:24,000 --> 01:75:28,000
how would you explain why they even decided to give those skills?

212
01:75:28,000 --> 01:75:30,000
Because surely the librarian got the librarian skills because she likes it.

213
01:75:30,000 --> 01:75:31,000
Right.

214
01:75:31,000 --> 01:75:40,000
So the secret record story is, look, we are confident that if we were to go and do a very detailed study of the person's life story,

215
01:75:40,000 --> 01:75:45,000
we would find that they had better opportunities for acquiring these librarian skills.

216
01:75:45,000 --> 01:75:50,000
So any of that could just be that you grew up in a family of librarians or readers,

217
01:75:50,000 --> 01:75:53,000
and other people grew up in a family of talkers, that kind of thing.

218
01:75:53,000 --> 01:75:56,000
So you have better opportunities for acquiring certain skills,

219
01:75:56,000 --> 01:76:01,000
and that explains the differences in what naive people have called preferences.

220
01:76:01,000 --> 01:76:06,000
And again, so a lot of it comes down to is just a slogan.

221
01:76:06,000 --> 01:76:08,000
It says, look, you can explain everything with preferences.

222
01:76:08,000 --> 01:76:13,000
It's not falsifiable, and so we shouldn't go and appeal preferences to explain anything.

223
01:76:13,000 --> 01:76:20,000
So I had a paper a while back, which was never cited by Heckman, but I'm not vindictive.

224
01:76:20,000 --> 01:76:27,000
I'm just glad that someone prominent is pushing forward the ball of truth, even if I get no credit for it.

225
01:76:27,000 --> 01:76:35,000
So anyway, what I point out in the paper is that there is a big literature in psychology on personality,

226
01:76:35,000 --> 01:76:39,000
and there are many things you can say about it.

227
01:76:39,000 --> 01:76:43,000
First of all, it seems like personality is a strong genetic component.

228
01:76:43,000 --> 01:76:46,000
It's not just something that you learn through experience.

229
01:76:46,000 --> 01:76:52,000
Secondly, personality is very predictive of a lot of outcomes,

230
01:76:52,000 --> 01:76:58,000
and it's very nicely maps on to what we think about preferences.

231
01:76:58,000 --> 01:77:02,000
So you find out what kind of personality librarians tend to have.

232
01:77:02,000 --> 01:77:05,000
They tend to be introverts, salesmen extroverts,

233
01:77:05,000 --> 01:77:10,000
and furthermore, it usually seems like they were this way from a very early age,

234
01:77:10,000 --> 01:77:13,000
long before they were planning a career or anything else,

235
01:77:13,000 --> 01:77:19,000
and furthermore, if you go and do Twitter adoption studies, you can see that this is not just appearance,

236
01:77:19,000 --> 01:77:23,000
but actually there is a strong genetic component to personality.

237
01:77:23,000 --> 01:77:26,000
So in this paper, I go over a lot of evidence from personality psychology

238
01:77:26,000 --> 01:77:30,000
that indicates there is a lot of preference heterogeneity.

239
01:77:30,000 --> 01:77:35,000
There are many people who just at a very gut level hate the stuff that you like,

240
01:77:35,000 --> 01:77:37,000
and like the stuff that you hate,

241
01:77:37,000 --> 01:77:43,000
and contrary to Stiglitz and Becker, there's no sign this can be reduced to their life experiences or anything like that.

242
01:77:43,000 --> 01:77:45,000
Not that life experiences don't matter at all,

243
01:77:45,000 --> 01:77:51,000
but that there's a lot of things about people that are there that seem to very deep set

244
01:77:51,000 --> 01:78:00,000
and have very little to do with any kind of a conscious or even subconscious decision to acquire a certain kind of capital.

245
01:78:00,000 --> 01:78:03,000
So, for example, criminals.

246
01:78:03,000 --> 01:78:06,000
Criminals have distinctive personalities.

247
01:78:06,000 --> 01:78:08,000
Like, they tend to be high in anger.

248
01:78:08,000 --> 01:78:10,000
Wow.

249
01:78:10,000 --> 01:78:14,000
And they tend to be low in empathy for others.

250
01:78:14,000 --> 01:78:18,000
And they also tend to be lazy and impulsive.

251
01:78:18,000 --> 01:78:24,000
And moreover, they were seen this way by adults in their lives from a very early age.

252
01:78:24,000 --> 01:78:28,000
And is it so surprising that the people with these preferences

253
01:78:28,000 --> 01:78:34,000
are more likely to pursue a career involving violence or robbery,

254
01:78:34,000 --> 01:78:37,000
and of course end up in jail a lot?

255
01:78:37,000 --> 01:78:39,000
Makes a lot of sense.

256
01:78:39,000 --> 01:78:41,000
So, I mean, there's been quite a bit of work on the criminal personality,

257
01:78:41,000 --> 01:78:44,000
which can be fixed very nicely in this, as well as on the genetics of criminality.

258
01:78:44,000 --> 01:78:49,000
As many people get creeped out by the idea that there could be genes for crime,

259
01:78:49,000 --> 01:78:50,000
but it really comes down to this.

260
01:78:50,000 --> 01:78:54,000
Do you think the genes could affect personality?

261
01:78:54,000 --> 01:78:56,000
How could they not?

262
01:78:56,000 --> 01:78:58,000
Do you think personality could affect crime?

263
01:78:58,000 --> 01:78:59,000
How could it not?

264
01:78:59,000 --> 01:79:01,000
Well, then of course genes affect crime.

265
01:79:01,000 --> 01:79:05,000
It's not that there's like the murder gene, but there's a bunch of genes

266
01:79:05,000 --> 01:79:08,000
which tend to lead to a bunch of other things, which tend to lead to a bunch of other things,

267
01:79:08,000 --> 01:79:13,000
and that's all we need to detect an effect of genes.

268
01:79:13,000 --> 01:79:15,000
All right.

269
01:79:15,000 --> 01:79:17,000
So questions about that?

270
01:79:17,000 --> 01:79:21,000
So some of the best uses of personality psychology are for occupational choice.

271
01:79:21,000 --> 01:79:23,000
It's not very predictive of income,

272
01:79:23,000 --> 01:79:26,000
but it's very predictive of the kinds of jobs you're likely to have.

273
01:79:26,000 --> 01:79:30,000
So you get a number of introverts in sales, very few.

274
01:79:30,000 --> 01:79:35,000
A number of extroverts working as librarians, again, very few.

275
01:79:35,000 --> 01:79:37,000
This was one that really struck me.

276
01:79:37,000 --> 01:79:42,000
So in the Myers-Briggs personality test, there's a thinking versus feeling dimension,

277
01:79:42,000 --> 01:79:46,000
and this is also the biggest gender gap,

278
01:79:46,000 --> 01:79:50,000
where men come out much more thinking and women come out much more feeling.

279
01:79:50,000 --> 01:79:52,000
But then if you go and look at males,

280
01:79:52,000 --> 01:79:57,000
there is a male occupation that is extremely feeling,

281
01:79:57,000 --> 01:80:00,000
where basically the men have a very feminine personality.


1
01:80:00,000 --> 01:80:04,000
What's the job?

2
01:80:04,000 --> 01:80:06,000
Priests and monks.

3
01:80:06,000 --> 01:80:07,000
Priests and monks.

4
01:80:07,000 --> 01:80:10,000
So male priests and monks have very feminine personalities

5
01:80:10,000 --> 01:80:13,000
according to statistical generalizations about personality.

6
01:80:13,000 --> 01:80:15,000
When I first read that, I was like, wow.

7
01:80:15,000 --> 01:80:17,000
That explains a lot.

8
01:80:17,000 --> 01:80:23,000
It explains why when I was in catechism and the priest was saying,

9
01:80:23,000 --> 01:80:24,000
you have to love everybody.

10
01:80:24,000 --> 01:80:26,000
I hate this guy every day.

11
01:80:26,000 --> 01:80:28,000
It made me so angry.

12
01:80:28,000 --> 01:80:33,000
I don't love those people.

13
01:80:33,000 --> 01:80:37,000
So that to me at least was a bit of a revelation.

14
01:80:37,000 --> 01:80:42,000
He was a man, but he had a very atypical personality for a man,

15
01:80:42,000 --> 01:80:45,000
which again to me just creeped me out.

16
01:80:45,000 --> 01:80:47,000
I'm just being honest.

17
01:80:47,000 --> 01:80:49,000
This is what I felt when I was ten years old.

18
01:80:49,000 --> 01:80:52,000
I should get credit for honesty.

19
01:80:52,000 --> 01:80:56,000
Isn't it a puzzle then why a lot of major religions don't allow women in clergy?

20
01:80:56,000 --> 01:80:58,000
Well, let's see.

21
01:80:58,000 --> 01:81:03,000
If you were a very feminine man and you wanted to crush the competition,

22
01:81:03,000 --> 01:81:09,000
so it seems like if they did allow women, then they might lose their jobs.

23
01:81:09,000 --> 01:81:13,000
Maybe that's not even a bad story, is it?

24
01:81:13,000 --> 01:81:15,000
You might say, well, if they were really that nice,

25
01:81:15,000 --> 01:81:18,000
they wouldn't want to crush other people.

26
01:81:18,000 --> 01:81:22,000
Of course I want to help people, but first I want it to be me.

27
01:81:22,000 --> 01:81:26,000
I don't want to be the one that has to do it.

28
01:81:26,000 --> 01:81:31,000
Then there's a really neat paper that talks about using personality tests

29
01:81:31,000 --> 01:81:34,000
to go and predict long-run life outcomes.

30
01:81:34,000 --> 01:81:36,000
This is a paper that says, well, it's not that impressive

31
01:81:36,000 --> 01:81:39,000
if you use current personality to predict current life outcomes.

32
01:81:39,000 --> 01:81:42,000
That's kind of obvious, and you might just say there's reverse causation.

33
01:81:42,000 --> 01:81:46,000
Like maybe for a jail, it makes you a 20th person.

34
01:81:46,000 --> 01:81:49,000
What this paper did is it did a meta-analysis of papers

35
01:81:49,000 --> 01:81:53,000
where you get personality at 18, and then you look at life outcomes

36
01:81:53,000 --> 01:81:57,000
like one or more decades later.

37
01:81:57,000 --> 01:82:03,000
The paper found a lot of neat results, saying personality is predictive of unemployment,

38
01:82:03,000 --> 01:82:09,000
predictive of divorce, predictive of suicide, predictive of being in jail.

39
01:82:09,000 --> 01:82:12,000
Again, this is not just saying that your current personality is predicting your current outcome.

40
01:82:12,000 --> 01:82:15,000
It's saying that you find out that you're depressed at 18,

41
01:82:15,000 --> 01:82:20,000
and this gives us some ability to predict whether you'll kill yourself by the time you're 40.

42
01:82:20,000 --> 01:82:25,000
That seems pretty impressive that you give someone a paper mental test,

43
01:82:25,000 --> 01:82:29,000
especially one where the questions seem kind of stupid, like I often feel sad.

44
01:82:29,000 --> 01:82:34,000
Very true, somewhat true, neither true nor false, somewhat false, very false.

45
01:82:34,000 --> 01:82:39,000
I know economists who just say these questions are meaningless and pointless.

46
01:82:39,000 --> 01:82:44,000
That's what you say, but they predict, so they're not meaningless and pointless.

47
01:82:44,000 --> 01:82:49,000
Do those, when they check up on people, do they reevaluate their personality as well?

48
01:82:49,000 --> 01:82:52,000
Normally not. Normally the whole goal is you get an early measure at 18,

49
01:82:52,000 --> 01:82:55,000
and then you just see what it tells you later in life.

50
01:82:55,000 --> 01:82:59,000
Again, because if they did later measures, then there's always the objection of

51
01:82:59,000 --> 01:83:04,000
life is causing personality changes rather than the other way around.

52
01:83:04,000 --> 01:83:07,000
Another interesting result in personality is that people,

53
01:83:07,000 --> 01:83:13,000
there are some clear patterns in how personality changes with age.

54
01:83:13,000 --> 01:83:17,000
In particular, as people get older, they become less open, more conscientious,

55
01:83:17,000 --> 01:83:20,000
more extroverted, more agreeable, and less erotic.

56
01:83:20,000 --> 01:83:22,000
Don't have to memorize that.

57
01:83:22,000 --> 01:83:24,000
Here's the interesting result.

58
01:83:24,000 --> 01:83:28,000
People generally substantially overestimate how much their personality has changed.

59
01:83:28,000 --> 01:83:32,000
If you give someone a personality test and then give them another one 20 years later,

60
01:83:32,000 --> 01:83:35,000
they'll give the test and think, my God, I'm a totally different person.

61
01:83:35,000 --> 01:83:37,000
They say, actually, we got your original test,

62
01:83:37,000 --> 01:83:41,000
and there's a 95% correspondence rate between these two test results.

63
01:83:41,000 --> 01:83:45,000
You didn't change very much.

64
01:83:50,000 --> 01:83:53,000
Now, expected utility anomalies.

65
01:83:53,000 --> 01:83:57,000
Like I said, expected utility theory puts some definite restrictions on

66
01:83:57,000 --> 01:84:00,000
what you will do under uncertainty.

67
01:84:00,000 --> 01:84:03,000
Many of these have been experimentally falsified.

68
01:84:03,000 --> 01:84:08,000
One thing is people seem to be risk-averse over utility rather than just wealth.

69
01:84:08,000 --> 01:84:15,000
The way that economics defines risk aversion is with this utility of wealth function.

70
01:84:15,000 --> 01:84:18,000
That's what we use to define risk aversion.

71
01:84:18,000 --> 01:84:25,000
Remember where if the negative of the second derivative over the first derivative is less than zero,

72
01:84:25,000 --> 01:84:27,000
then we say you're risk-averse.

73
01:84:27,000 --> 01:84:29,000
Remember that.

74
01:84:29,000 --> 01:84:36,000
But in expected utility theory, it doesn't make sense to be risk-averse over utility itself.

75
01:84:36,000 --> 01:84:37,000
We've already factored that in.

76
01:84:37,000 --> 01:84:40,000
Yet, in the real world, if you tell people, look,

77
01:84:40,000 --> 01:84:46,000
suppose you could either have a.6 probability of having a happiness of 10 on a 0 to 10 scale,

78
01:84:46,000 --> 01:84:49,000
or you could have 5 for sure.

79
01:84:49,000 --> 01:84:52,000
A lot of people will say, I'd rather have 5 for sure.

80
01:84:52,000 --> 01:84:54,000
Unhappiness, not wealth.

81
01:84:54,000 --> 01:84:58,000
They're not even maximizing their expected happiness.

82
01:84:58,000 --> 01:85:01,000
There's some kind of risk aversion over the happiness.

83
01:85:01,000 --> 01:85:10,000
It's like I'd rather have certainty of moderate happiness than a gamble where there's a good chance I'll be super happy,

84
01:85:10,000 --> 01:85:14,000
but there's also a risk that I won't be happy at all.

85
01:85:14,000 --> 01:85:21,000
So that is not in the theory, and yet it seems that it comes out of observation.

86
01:85:21,000 --> 01:85:26,000
Another thing is many people are risk-averse relative to a reference point.

87
01:85:26,000 --> 01:85:29,000
So suppose Bill Gates plays poker.

88
01:85:29,000 --> 01:85:36,000
In the real world, if Bill Gates loses, he loses when the stakes are like a dollar hand.

89
01:85:36,000 --> 01:85:42,000
Can you believe that Bill Gates might actually have a worse day because he lost at poker?

90
01:85:42,000 --> 01:85:48,000
Like playing for him is an amount of money almost too small to imagine.

91
01:85:48,000 --> 01:85:50,000
Can you believe that he might actually have a bad day?

92
01:85:50,000 --> 01:85:53,000
Yeah, right?

93
01:85:53,000 --> 01:85:57,000
So I will say that I have done a bunch of debates.

94
01:85:57,000 --> 01:85:59,000
Some of them there's a vote, and some of them there's not a vote.

95
01:85:59,000 --> 01:86:02,000
And you know what? I like the ones without a vote better.

96
01:86:02,000 --> 01:86:10,000
Because the ones that have a vote, there's a chance that I will feel like a loser.

97
01:86:10,000 --> 01:86:13,000
Whereas the ones without a vote, I always win this.

98
01:86:13,000 --> 01:86:16,000
I've never failed to win a debate without a vote.

99
01:86:16,000 --> 01:86:21,000
Whereas the ones that have votes, then on the one hand, I'm like, well, I definitely have the better argument.

100
01:86:21,000 --> 01:86:25,000
I don't know what's wrong with these people, but still, I have lost.

101
01:86:25,000 --> 01:86:29,000
So given that, and this is where there's no stakes.

102
01:86:29,000 --> 01:86:35,000
The payment that you receive for being in the debate has nothing to do with whether you won or lost.

103
01:86:35,000 --> 01:86:41,000
And most of the time, a month later, no one on earth remembers whether you won or lost except, of course, you.

104
01:86:41,000 --> 01:86:45,000
I know what the votes were for every debate I've been in, I think.

105
01:86:45,000 --> 01:86:49,000
That never goes away, especially the losses.

106
01:86:49,000 --> 01:87:00,000
And this is true even though I know that there are some things that are logically relevant that strongly predict when you're losing.

107
01:87:00,000 --> 01:87:09,000
In particular, if the resolution is something that almost no one would agree with initially, you almost always win.

108
01:87:09,000 --> 01:87:14,000
I mean, imagine this. Suppose that initially 100% of people vote against you.

109
01:87:14,000 --> 01:87:16,000
Can you lose that debate?

110
01:87:16,000 --> 01:87:20,000
The worst scenario is you tie, because you gain no votes.

111
01:87:20,000 --> 01:87:24,000
And if you persuade one person, you're declared the winner of the debate.

112
01:87:24,000 --> 01:87:33,000
So you can game the system and just try to lobby for ridiculous statements that almost no one would agree with,

113
01:87:33,000 --> 01:87:36,000
and then you just try to win a few people over to them, and then you win all your debates.

114
01:87:36,000 --> 01:87:48,000
The last debate that I did, they did have a vote there at Kenyishek. The resolution was, I think, the education system is a waste of time and money.

115
01:87:48,000 --> 01:87:54,000
And before he looked at me and said, well, I don't think there's any way that you could lose this debate, Brian.

116
01:87:54,000 --> 01:87:58,000
And I'm like, hmm, well, I respect you all the more because you realize that.

117
01:87:58,000 --> 01:88:01,000
But still, I'm going to like beating you.

118
01:88:01,000 --> 01:88:08,000
And yes, I did beat him, and it wasn't hard because initially only 15% of people agreed with me, and then I got it up to 30.

119
01:88:08,000 --> 01:88:13,000
So wow. I doubled the share of people who agreed with me. Fantastic.

120
01:88:13,000 --> 01:88:19,000
All right. Another thing. So a choice is not linear in probability, as expected utility predicts.

121
01:88:19,000 --> 01:88:24,000
